                    {
                        id on-conflict-columns
                        title "A conflict target names a composite key in one call"
                        kind @feat
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-10-05T13:28:58.739977Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Filed from composite-key-writes (870d0916), whose survey note asked whether this belonged there; it is a pgorm-query surface addition, so it is its own node. Arbitrating an upsert on a composite key today reads OnConflict::column(tenant_id).and_columns([id]) (tests/composite_key_tests.rs upsert_on_the_whole_key); there is no OnConflict::columns([..]). The likely reason it was never added: ConflictTarget is non-empty by construction (pgorm-query/src/query/on_conflict.rs, column() at ~:288, and_columns() at ~:444 documents that an empty iterator leaves the target as it was), and a columns(iter) constructor over an arbitrary iterator could be handed nothing, which per [dec:pgorm:invalid-states-unrepresentable] must not construct an empty target. Options to weigh: a constructor over a non-empty shape (an array of length >= 1 via const generics, or (first, rest)), or an entity-level helper that names a PrimaryKey's columns (Insert::on_conflict_do_nothing already builds exactly that target internally, src/query/insert.rs). Acceptance: one call names a multi-column target; an empty one does not compile; the curated export earns its sql.surface line; a live upsert on a two-column key uses it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-10-05T21:18:00.717399Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "composite-key-refusals (d982a21d) landed the key type this node's note asked for. pgorm-query's TableKey<K> (K = Primary | Unique) is a non-empty, ordered column tuple, built from one column, a tuple of one to twelve (IntoTableKey) or TableKey::new(first).cols(rest). It has no impl for anything that could be empty. The operator's decision on that node says the key type should also suit this node's ON CONFLICT target, which suggests OnConflict taking IntoTableKey<K> (any kind) for a one-call composite target. That reuses the non-emptiness by construction rather than a const-generic array."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-10-05T22:24:59.395289Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-10-05T22:34:20.144563Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Shape chosen 2026-10-06: OnConflict::columns(impl IntoKeyColumns) -> ConflictTarget. IntoKeyColumns is a new public trait in pgorm-query/src/table/key.rs, for one IntoName column or a tuple of 1 to 12. Its method returns (first, rest), so no impl, downstream ones included, can yield an empty list. IntoTableKey<K> is now a blanket over IntoKeyColumns, plus TableKey<K> itself, so the tuple machinery is shared, not copied. The target does not take IntoTableKey<Unique> directly. A TableKey carries a name, INCLUDE, NULLS NOT DISTINCT and deferrability, which an inference target cannot render and would silently drop. A deferrable key is refused as an arbiter anyway (55000). OnConflict::column(c) stays as the one-column start, and ConflictTarget::and_columns(iter) stays for computed lists, so the ConflictArbiter/ConflictConstraint typestate is unchanged. An empty target does not compile: arrays, (), slices and Vec are not IntoKeyColumns (compile_fail E0277). Repeated columns are not refused, because the server accepts them (see table-key-distinct-columns). The ORM's own helper, Insert::on_conflict_do_nothing, builds from PrimaryKey::iter(), a computed list, so it keeps column(first).and_columns(rest)."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-10-06T12:15:11.139777Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

