                    {
                        id table-key-distinct-columns
                        title "A table key cannot name the same column twice"
                        kind @fix
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-10-05T22:00:12.634924Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Found while landing composite-key-refusals (d982a21d): TableKey still accepts a repeated column, e.g. .primary_key((a, a)) or TableKey::new(a).col(a), which PostgreSQL refuses with 42701 (column appears twice in key; verify live, for unique and primary, inline and ALTER ADD). Decide between making it unrepresentable or refused per [dec:pgorm:invalid-states-unrepresentable] (check how TableKey's other invariants are kept and what is cheap at tuple-conversion time), and dropping or deduplicating it, which is a silent reinterpretation and probably wrong. The same question applies to the ON CONFLICT target if on-conflict-columns adopts TableKey's shape first: a repeated inference column there may be accepted by the server, so check before sharing the rule."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-10-05T22:05:12.970275Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Checked live on PostgreSQL 16.15 while landing on-conflict-columns, 2026-10-06. The server ACCEPTS a repeated column in an ON CONFLICT inference target. Inference matches an index by its column set, so repeats collapse: against PRIMARY KEY (a, b), ON CONFLICT (a, b, a) DO UPDATE and ON CONFLICT (b, a, b) DO NOTHING both arbitrate the key. Against UNIQUE (a), ON CONFLICT (a, a) and (a, a, a) arbitrate it too. ON CONFLICT (a, a) against PRIMARY KEY (a, b) is refused, but with 42P10 (no matching constraint) because {a} is not {a, b}, not because of the repeat. So the distinct-columns rule must NOT be shared with the conflict target: refusing a repeat there would be stricter than the server. on-conflict-columns gave the target a sibling trait, IntoKeyColumns, which TableKey's IntoTableKey is now built on, rather than TableKey itself. If this node enforces distinctness at tuple-conversion time, it belongs in the IntoTableKey/TableKey layer, not in IntoKeyColumns, or OnConflict::columns inherits it. The table-key side is confirmed too: PRIMARY KEY (a, a) gives 42701 \"column \\\"a\\\" appears twice in primary key constraint\", and UNIQUE (a, b, a) gives 42701 \"... appears twice in unique constraint\" (both inline in CREATE TABLE; ALTER ADD not yet checked)."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-10-08T15:41:48.077043Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-10-08T15:46:05.145097Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Decided 2026-10-08, per [dec:pgorm:invalid-states-unrepresentable] and [dec:pgorm:no-panic]: the builder writes a repeated key column as given and the server refuses it; it is neither ruled out by type nor refused by the key, nor deduplicated. Unrepresentable is impossible: two columns of one type (two variants of one Column enum, two runtime Names) are two values no type tells apart. Refused by the key would make col, cols, every tuple conversion and every embedder (primary_key, unique, add_primary_key, add_unique) return a Result, an error path on every key a program declares for the one mistake the statement's execution reports by name (42701); pgorm executes DDL as its text, so execution is the fallible boundary a DDL statement has, as no-panic's consequence says. Deduplicating would declare a key other than the one written, unseen. Codegen reads keys at a fallible boundary of its own and refuses a repeat by name: the DDL bridge's PRIMARY KEY / UNIQUE (\"statement <n>: table `t` names column `c` twice in one key\"), a unique index folding into a constraint (\"column `c` named twice on unique index `i`\", since the server creates the index but not the constraint), and the transform gate for a caller-built statement (\"table `t`: a key names column `c` twice\"). Before this a repeat generated a PrimaryKey enum with one variant twice (did not compile), and UNIQUE (a, a) marked a unique on its own through the column set. Live, identical on 18.6 and 19beta4: PRIMARY KEY (a, a), UNIQUE (a, b, a), ALTER ADD PRIMARY KEY / UNIQUE each 42701; (p, p WITHOUT OVERLAPS) 42701 even without btree_gist; INCLUDE (a, b, b) on key (a) accepted (indkey 1 1 2 2), so the included columns may repeat; CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ON t (a, a) accepted; a foreign key's referencing list may repeat (FOREIGN KEY (a, a) REFERENCES p (x, y) accepted) while the referenced list may not (42830). Python mirrors Rust and is unchanged."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-10-08T17:00:43.03556Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-05T09:33:28.780356Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator 2026-10-05, asked \"what about composite primary keys? SeaORM couldn't handle that at all\": a survey at c24fea1e (24 existing tests run, plus live probes since deleted) found the core works end to end — tuple keys of arity 1-12, find_by_id/delete_by_id, ActiveModel insert/update/delete, insert returning the key tuple, upsert on the full key, composite FKs, find_related both ways, a composite junction for many-to-many, Linked over 2-3 hops, load_one/load_many/load_many_via batching as (a,b) IN (...), graph decode and grouping, keyset cursors with every key column as a tiebreak, paginate/count, schema-gen PRIMARY KEY (a,b) plus composite FOREIGN KEY, and codegen's tuple keys and junction detection. The operator chose to build all four gap groups the survey found, as the four children of this node. The ordering is in deps: generated-column first because it reshapes how the entity macro records auto_increment, which writes and refusals both touch; edge-coverage last because it tests what the other three settle."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

