                        text "Operator-approved design (2026-09-04): prql!(\"from invoice | filter total > $1 | take 5\", min_total) expands at build time to (&'static str SQL, Values). The macro compiles the PRQL text through prqlc (text path this time — prql_to_pl -> pl_to_rq -> rq_to_sql, Postgres dialect), validates the emitted SQL through libpg_query exactly as sql! does, and reads the Param nodes from the parse to enforce as COMPILE ERRORS: arity (placeholder count vs argument count, naming the missing $N), contiguity ($1,$3 with no $2 refused), and PRQL's own take-$N rejection surfacing at build. $N reuse = one argument bound once; arguments convert via Into<Value> in placeholder order. S-strings in the text are the caller's escape hatch and still pass the oracle (it validates the final SQL). What cannot be checked at build: value-vs-column typing (no catalog) — runtime prepare + verify::<M> cover it, stated in the rule. Placement: pgorm-sql-macro (the proc-macro crate gains its own exact-pinned prqlc build-dep); whether prql! rides the existing sql-macro feature or the crate's re-export becomes unconditional is implementer judgment — note the operator's permanence posture on prqlc (pipeline-permanent) and justify. Sibling of ergo.pipeline-v1: text form for static queries, typed pipeline for composition/dynamic; the verdict that predicted the macro \"falls out as a byproduct\" is ergo.pipeline-api's."
                    }

