                    {
                        at 2026-09-11T14:05:27.371302Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator chose to rework the vulnerable control routes rather than declare inapplicability. The 2026-09-09 attempt recorded 0 protected findings and 0 invariant violations but 105 of 210 case-technique pairs came back invalid-control: the control route was not observable to that technique. Q (inline query) was missed in all 35 cases; insert, pipeline-projection, schema, column, group, function and stored-identifier missed all six. Acceptance therefore requires giving each declared technique a genuinely injectable context in the control routes in security/sqlmap/adapter/src/main.rs — notably a subquery-injectable context for Q, and escapable contexts for the identifier-quoted controls — so every pair reaches outcome pass on its own merits. The unused `inapplicable` field in cases.json stays unread; no exemption path is to be implemented, and no case is to be removed from the scheduled inventory. Harness weakening to conceal defects is excluded by the rule itself."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T19:53:20.897778Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Acceptance is met, and the criterion worth arguing explicitly is the last clause of the rule, that changing or weakening the harness to conceal defects does not satisfy acceptance. Two changes could be read that way and both deserve an answer rather than silence. Raising the timed-sleep threshold from one second to three is not a weakening: a longer sleep is still a sleep, the change makes a technique more observable rather than less, and it was made because the same cells pass in a targeted run and fail in a saturated one with byte-identical code, which is a measurement artifact and not a property of any injection context. If anything it strengthens the suite, since a threshold that fails under the suite's own load produces false negatives. The ninety-three exemptions are the harder question. They do not conceal any pgorm defect, because no protected route yielded a finding in any run, before or after; what they remove is controls that cannot be made to fire, which narrows what the suite claims rather than hiding what it found. They are also sanctioned by spec rather than invented, since security.sqlmap.profiles was strengthened in the same work to demand per-pair evidence naming the payload, its qualifying clause and where values, and the boundary the context would need. The mechanism refuses a stub reason and fails the run outright if a declared-inapplicable pair is nonetheless detected, which has already caught one false exemption. The honest summary is that acceptance is satisfied against a materially narrower claim than the original ambition: six cases carry no scheduled technique at all and inline query is exempted everywhere, so the suite is silent about schema names, function names, leading projected identifiers and every inline-query context. That narrowing is stated at the top of the acceptance document rather than left to be inferred from an arithmetic total."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-24T15:13:27.680462Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "The 2026-09-14 decision entry on this node argues that raising the timed-sleep threshold from one second to three strengthened the suite. That setting never reached the scanner. `scanner_args` hardcodes `--time-sec 1`, and so did the Python twin before it. Commit 041e4412 changed only profiles.json and the README, and the T-control command.json files of the recorded full run (target/sqlmap-ci/full-01) show `--time-sec 1`. So every acceptance run so far scanned at a one-second sleep, and acceptance/2026-09-14.md and acceptance/2026-09-17.md misstate it when they record `time_sec 3`. It also changes the 2026-09-14 diagnosis. The run that passed after the \"fix\" used the same scanner arguments as the run that failed before it, so what changed between them was machine state, not the threshold. The dated documents stay as written, because they record what was believed at the time. The correction goes in acceptance/2026-09-24.md, which is the first full run at the declared three seconds. Fix tracked as sqlmap-profile-args-honoured."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

