                    {
                        id sqlmap.controls.verify
                        title "A full profile detects every scheduled control"
                        kind @test
                        deps (sqlmap.controls.literal sqlmap.controls.inapplicable)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T15:57:20.922319Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Runtime prerequisites established before the verify run, so the node does not lose time discovering them. The pinned scanner is already extracted at target/sqlmap-cache/scanner/sqlmap-d486742eec47ba96940d35bf2dc176f60868efdd with sqlmap.py present, so no download is needed and the run can stay offline. The harness hard-fails when the interpreter's version differs from the pin in pins.json, which is 3.14.4, and this machine's default python3 is 3.11.6, so the runner must be given --python explicitly. Three interpreters on disk satisfy the pin: /opt/homebrew/bin/python3.14, target/generative-build/venv/bin/python and target/python-check/bin/python. Docker is present at server version 29.4. The full profile is 126 scheduled pairs after exemptions, each pair running a control scan and a protected scan, so roughly 252 scanner invocations at a 240 second per-case timeout; the recorded CI deadline for a full run is 300 minutes, so this needs to run unattended rather than inline. The adapter must be built first with cargo build --locked --bins, and the run wants --direct-regressions so the SQL security regressions are exercised against the same disposable database."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T16:59:49.446376Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T17:35:26.408385Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Partway through the first full run the failures split cleanly along one axis, and the split is itself informative. Of fifteen non-passes at the halfway point, nine involve boolean, error or union, which confirm by response content rather than by timing, and every one of those nine was predicted by the contexts analysis: pipeline-projection, column and group failing boolean, and enum and enum-ddl failing boolean, error and union. That is a strong independent validation of the analysis, since those predictions were derived by reading payload and boundary XML before any scan was run. The other six involve stacked queries or time-based blind, both of which confirm by timing, and the machine is carrying a load average between six and fourteen from an unrelated compilation. One of those six, contains-S, is proven to be a load artifact because sqlmap said so in its own words, reporting considerable lagging detected in connection responses and suggesting a higher time-sec. The profile pins time-sec at one second, which is aggressive even on an idle machine. Two more, insert-S and insert-T, contradict the reshape node's claim that those cells now fire, though insert-S completed cleanly with return code zero and fifty-six requests and no lag warning, which argues for genuine non-detection rather than load. The remaining two, enum-T and enum-ddl-T, were predicted to fail anyway. The practical consequence is that this run can settle the non-timing half of the matrix but not the timing half, and a trustworthy verdict on stacked and time-based techniques needs a quiet machine and probably a longer time-sec. Raising time-sec does not weaken the test, since a longer sleep is still a sleep, and it is a harness tuning knob rather than a change to what is being detected."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T17:36:52.419632Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "The run will not pass, and the reason is a gap between the two preceding nodes rather than a surprise. Ten scheduled pairs are in a state of being neither repaired nor exempted: pipeline-projection, column, group and stored-identifier failing boolean, and enum and enum-ddl failing boolean, error and union. The reshape node examined all of them and declined to change their controls, with a sound argument in each case, chiefly that error and time-based payloads carry no comment at level three and so can only fire in a genuine truth slot, never escaping a double-quoted identifier. But declining to reshape is only half a decision. Each of those cells then needed either an evidence-backed exemption or an accepted plan to fix it, and neither was recorded, so they simply stayed scheduled and are now failing. That is the honest state and it is exactly what a verification run is for: it caught an omission that the unit tests could not, because the unit tests only check that declared exemptions are well formed, not that undeclared cells can actually pass. The remedy is a decision per cell, not more code. Either the argument in the contexts analysis is strong enough to justify exemptions for these ten, in which case they should be written with the same evidence standard as the eighty-three already there, or the identifier cases should take the trailing-slot reshape the analysis suggests, accepting that it changes what those cases test and needs the corresponding exemption swap. The suite cannot claim a passing full profile until that decision is made and recorded."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T17:40:16.155505Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "The first full run reports source_unchanged false, and the cause is mine rather than the code under test. Comparing the before and after source records shows identical content digests over eleven hundred and seventy four files with zero files differing; the only thing that moved is the recorded git HEAD, because a plan bookkeeping commit landed while the scan was in flight. This is worth fixing in the harness rather than merely avoiding. The content hash already excludes plan and dot-nplan paths on purpose, so that plan activity cannot invalidate a run, but the HEAD comparison sits outside that exclusion and defeats it. A run whose every tracked file is byte-identical from start to finish has not had its subject change underneath it, and should not be reported as though it had. The narrow fix is to treat the run as unchanged when the content digests match, and to report the HEAD movement as information rather than as a failure condition, since HEAD is useful provenance but is not what the check is protecting against. Until that is fixed the operational rule is simply not to commit anything while a profile is running, which is a poor constraint given a full run takes over an hour."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T17:42:00.124774Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "First full run after the control rework, completed: 110 pass, 16 invalid-control and 1 incomplete across 127 scheduled pairs, with 83 declared inapplicable, so every one of the 210 cells is accounted for. The run does not pass, and that is the correct verdict. Three results are worth separating from the failure count because they are the substantive security findings. Zero pairs came back vulnerable, meaning sqlmap found no injection in any protected route exercising real pgorm APIs. The direct SQL security regressions passed against the same disposable database. And falsified_exemptions is empty, so none of the 83 evidence-backed exemptions was contradicted by a technique actually firing, which is the self-check that keeps the exemption mechanism honest. Against that, the rework demonstrably worked where it was applied: update-value and cast pass every scheduled technique, and insert now passes boolean, error and union where previously all six missed. The seventeen failures fall into three groups. Ten are the cells the reshape node examined and declined to change without recording an exemption, namely boolean against pipeline-projection, column, group and stored-identifier, and boolean, error and union against enum and enum-ddl; these are an omission rather than a surprise and need a decision per cell. Four are timing-dependent and were measured on a machine carrying a load average between six and fourteen from an unrelated compilation, namely insert stacked and time-based, contains time-based, and stored-value stacked, with contains stacked recorded as incomplete because sqlmap itself reported considerable lagging in connection responses. Those four need a quiet machine before they can be trusted either way. Two, enum and enum-ddl time-based, were predicted to fail regardless. The non-timing failures match the contexts analysis exactly, which is a meaningful independent validation of predictions made by reading payload XML before any scan ran."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T18:19:17.658221Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "The load hypothesis was largely wrong and the retained scanner output says so directly. Of the four timing-dependent failures I attributed to machine contention, three — insert stacked, contains time-based and stored-value stacked — each completed with return code zero, no errors, sensible request counts and durations between eleven and twenty-one seconds, and no lag warning. More decisively, each one's scanner.json carries the message that all tested parameters do not appear to be injectable and suggests increasing level or risk. That is sqlmap reporting a level gate, not a timing failure, and it puts these three in the same category as the other level-gated cells rather than in a category of their own. Only contains stacked was genuinely load, and that one is identifiable because sqlmap reported considerable lagging in connection responses in its own words. The correction matters for what happens next: a quiet-machine re-run will not change these three, because the machine was never the problem. They need the same treatment as every other unreachable cell, which is either an evidence-backed exemption citing the level-three boundary catalogue, or a control reshape that gives the technique a reachable slot, with the shape-versus-structure question asked cell by cell rather than by family. It also means my earlier framing in the 2026-09-14 acceptance document, which grouped four cells as untrustworthy under load, overstates the load problem and should be corrected when that document is revised."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T19:18:40.848355Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Correcting my own correction, this time on measurement rather than on reading. I first attributed the timing failures to machine load, then reversed that after finding sqlmap's message about parameters not appearing injectable and a suggestion to raise level or risk, and concluded they were level-gated. That reversal was wrong. The message is what sqlmap emits whenever it finds nothing at all, so it cannot distinguish a genuine level gate from a timing signal drowned by load, and I treated a generic string as a diagnosis. Running the three disputed cases plus contains as a targeted subset on an otherwise quiet machine, load average two point two five, every one of the fifteen scheduled cells passed, including insert stacked, contains time-based, contains stacked and stored-value stacked, with no exemptions added and no control reshaped. The code was identical to the run where they failed. The conclusion is that the full profile defeats itself: two hundred and fifty-four scanner invocations at time-sec one saturate the machine badly enough that the very techniques confirming by sleep stop being observable, and the failures are an artifact of the run's own scale rather than of any injection context. That is a harness scalability defect worth naming, because a suite whose result depends on how many cases it is running cannot be trusted either way. The remedy is to raise time-sec so the sleep stands clear of scheduling noise; a longer sleep is still a sleep, so this tunes sensitivity rather than weakening what is detected. The earlier enum stacked evidence that I used to argue against load was consistent with this all along, since it came from a small targeted run rather than from the saturated full profile."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T19:51:10.201466Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "The full profile passes: 117 of 117 scheduled pairs detected in their controls, zero protected routes yielding a finding, zero falsified exemptions, direct SQL security regressions passing in the same run, no cleanup errors, source byte-identical start to end, and no subset. Ninety-three further pairs are declared inapplicable with retraceable evidence, so all two hundred and ten cells are accounted for. Against the 2026-09-09 attempt, which recorded one hundred and five invalid controls, every previously failing cell now either passes or carries an evidenced exemption. The decisive change was raising the timed-sleep threshold from one second to three: insert stacked and insert time-based, the two cells I argued longest about and wrongly declared level-gated, pass with no code change at all, and so do contains stacked and contains time-based. That confirms in the run that matters what the targeted subset had already shown, namely that the suite's own load was masking the techniques that confirm by sleep. Worth recording for whoever revisits this: the number to distrust is not the pass count but the ninety-three exemptions, because they rest on reading the scanner's payload and boundary definitions rather than on measurement, and three exemptions already filed as structural have been repaired after turning out to depend on the control's shape. Six cases now carry no scheduled technique at all, so the suite makes no detection claim about schema names, function names or leading projected identifiers; that is stated at the top of the acceptance document rather than left in an arithmetic total."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T19:51:44.825902Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id sqlmap.controls.inapplicable
                        title "An exempted pair cites the evidence that proves it unreachable"
                        kind @feat
                        deps (sqlmap.controls.contexts)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T15:46:22.448952Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Correction to the first pass: the insert case was over-exempted, and this is the exact failure mode the mechanism exists to prevent. All six techniques were exempted on the grounds of a VALUES arity wall, but the written reason said the control puts the input in the third of four VALUES columns, which is a fact about the control we happen to have rather than about what any faithful control could do. The contexts analysis says as much itself: its remedy section names moving the injected literal to the final VALUES column, or an INSERT SELECT WHERE form, and either remains an honest INSERT control. Exempting a shape problem as though it were structural is how an exemption mechanism quietly erodes coverage. Boolean, error, stacked and time were therefore returned to scheduled work for insert, leaving only union, which genuinely has no set-operation position in a DML statement, and inline query, which is unreachable everywhere. The arithmetic moves from 88 exempt and 122 scheduled to 84 and 126. Two cases still carry no scheduled technique at all, schema and function, and they deserve the same scrutiny before being accepted, because a suite making no detection claim at all about schema-name or function-name handling should say so loudly rather than let a zero pass unnoticed. The pinned invocation counts in the runner tests caught the change immediately, which is what those pins are for."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-14T16:59:29.464321Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T12:24:14.205147Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The breakdown follows injection context rather than case, because context is what decides which techniques can reach a control. Reading the thirty-five control routes in adapter/src/main.rs, they fall into three classes. String literal contexts interpolate into a quoted value, as in WHERE name='{input}' — these cover insert, update-value, update-guard, delete, tenant-or, empty-in, empty-or, parameters, the four LIKE cases, stored-value and the default arm, and they are the ones already detected by most techniques. Quoted identifier contexts interpolate inside double quotes, as in SELECT \"{input}\" FROM items — schema, table, column, group, pipeline-projection, stored-identifier, order, alias, graph-alias, enum and enum-ddl. These are where detection collapses, because escaping a double-quoted identifier leaves you needing the result to remain a valid identifier position. Unquoted contexts interpolate bare text into a keyword or numeric slot — function, cast, direction, limit and offset. The measured failures line up with those classes: inline query was missed in all thirty-five cases, all six techniques were missed for insert, pipeline-projection, schema, column, group, function and stored-identifier, and the remaining misses cluster in the identifier and unquoted groups. Inline query is the one technique that fails everywhere, and it needs a position where a subquery can be planted; limit and offset can host one directly since LIMIT (SELECT 1) is valid, while an identifier position needs the control to close the quote and open a projection. Worth noting that some of this may be about presenting a boundary shape sqlmap already recognises from its own boundaries list rather than an exotic one it has no payload for, so the contexts node should establish per technique what is actually required before any route is rewritten."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T13:57:14.945398Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The operator's earlier choice to rework control routes rather than declare inapplicability is reversed, on evidence gathered since. Reworking cannot reach 210 of 210 because most of the 105 invalid-control pairs are structurally unreachable rather than merely badly written. The decisive fact, verified directly rather than taken from the analysis: inline query ships eight payloads and every one is where=3, REPLACE, and exactly one boundary in sqlmap's whole catalogue serves where=3, carrying an empty prefix and an empty suffix. Inline query therefore cannot escape a quote at any level, only replace a value already sitting unquoted, and because it confirms by grepping the response that value must also be reflected back. pgorm binds values and quotes identifiers, so it never emits an unquoted reflected projection, and no faithful control for inline query exists anywhere in this suite. That alone accounts for thirty-five pairs permanently. The same structural argument removes union against INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE, which offer no set-operation position, union after a terminal clause such as LIMIT or ORDER BY, where nothing may syntactically follow, and boolean, error and time against a bare object-name tail such as FROM quoted-name or AS quoted-name, which has no truth slot to hang a predicate on. The only way to make those fire is to give the control a different shape from the protected route it is twinned with, at which point its detection says nothing about pgorm in the real context and the control stops being a control. The chosen path is therefore declared inapplicability, but with a materially higher bar than the unread empty field that exists today: each exemption must cite the specific boundary or payload evidence that proves the cell unreachable, so a reviewer can check the claim rather than accept it. Everything not exempted must then be detected, with no partial credit. Also confirmed while checking: the full profile runs at level 3 and the harness passes extending=N, which declines sqlmap's offer to escalate to levels 4 and 5, so every identifier-escape, pre-WHERE, table-name and dollar-quoted boundary is out of reach by configuration rather than by nature."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T16:57:26.605842Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Twice now an exemption filed as structurally impossible turned out to depend on the control's shape rather than on SQL grammar, and both were caught, which is worth recording as a pattern rather than two incidents. The first was insert boolean, error, stacked and time, exempted on a VALUES arity wall whose written reason described the third of four columns, a fact about our control and not about what any INSERT could do; returning them to scheduled work was a judgement call made by reading the reason. The second was insert union, exempted on the argument that a DML statement offers no set-operation position. That is true of INSERT VALUES but false of INSERT SELECT WHERE, and once the control was reshaped to the latter the inner SELECT's set-operation position became union-injectable through RETURNING. This one was not caught by judgement but by the mechanism itself: a declared-inapplicable pair that the scanner detects fails the run, so the false exemption could not survive contact with a real scan and had to be removed. The lesson for any future exemption is that the burden of proof is about the context class, not the specific statement we happen to have written, and that a reason mentioning a detail of the current control is a signal the claim is shape-dependent. Exemptions now stand at 83 with 127 scheduled, and the three reshaped controls are update-value moving its literal from the SET assignment into the WHERE, cast embedding the CAST in a WHERE so the unquoted type closes into a truth slot, and insert becoming a guarded INSERT SELECT WHERE. Each protected twin was updated alongside its control so the pair remains a matched twin rather than drifting into testing two different things."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T18:15:45.760877Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The shape-dependent exemption pattern has now appeared three times, and the third instance is the most instructive because the wrong claim was mine. Briefing the exemption work I asserted that the level-three double-quote boundaries are usable only by the comment-bearing union and stacked techniques. That is false for boolean, which carries two generic-comment tests at level two and a double-dash stacked test at level three, and the two cells no bullet in my argument actually covered, enum boolean and enum union, turned out to be exactly the repairable ones. They failed only because the closed identifier sat in front of an enum-typed projection, where AND is a type error and every union marker except NULL is refused against the enum; moving the same cast into a WHERE filter on an enum column puts the identifier in a truth slot and both now fire, scanner-confirmed. That is a detail of the shape, not of the context class, which is the same signature as the two insert cells before it. The lesson is that an argument for unreachability has to cover each cell individually rather than by family resemblance, and that the person writing the brief is as capable of the error as the person implementing it. A second finding settles an open question from the first full run: enum stacked and enum-ddl stacked both pass, and stacked queries confirm by timing, so the machine can observe a sleep. The time-based failures on those two cases are therefore structural rather than load artifacts, and they are now exempted on the same evidence as their error-based siblings. That leaves the load hypothesis applying only to contains stacked, where sqlmap reported lagging in its own words, and leaves insert stacked, contains time-based and stored-value stacked genuinely open. Six cases now carry no scheduled technique at all: pipeline-projection, schema, column, group, function and stored-identifier. The suite makes no detection claim about any of them, and that has to be stated prominently rather than buried in an arithmetic total."
                    }
                )
            }

