                    {
                        at 2026-09-13T18:20:51.278442Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Measured groundwork for the million-program stress demonstration. Construction-only generation runs at about 989 programs per second single-threaded on this machine, so one million is roughly seventeen minutes of compute and needs no parallelism to be practical. The catch is distinctness, which the rule demands explicitly: a sample of 4000 programs cycling the fifteen families produced only 3995 distinct digests, three collision groups, every one of them in the active family, where different indices generated byte-identical programs. At that rate a naive run of one million indices would fall short of one million distinct programs by something on the order of a thousand. The harness therefore has to deduplicate by program digest and keep generating until the distinct count reaches the target, rather than assuming index count equals program count. Holding one million digests is cheap enough at roughly sixteen megabytes if a prefix is stored rather than the full hex string. The same run must record that these are construction-only programs that never reached PostgreSQL or an oracle, which the runner's per-class counting already makes structural, and must run under one unchanged native build identity with zero per-program builds, which the executor already asserts by refusing to run when the loaded extension hash differs from the recorded build."
                    }

