    {
        id pipeline-join-distinct-order
        title "A joined, deduplicated pipeline compiles to one projection order"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-11T14:55:36.854441Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "The same Pipeline value, compiled repeatedly without rebuilding, emits its projection columns in one of two orders chosen at random per compilation. Shape is a join against a nested-pipeline source followed by distinct; neither stage alone is unstable. CTE names are byte-identical between renderings, so only the deduplicated projection order moves: the left relation's columns and the joined relation's columns swap group position. Reproduced through the Rust API with no Python in the process, so this is not the binding — src/pipeline/ holds no HashSet or HashMap, and the projection expansion happens inside prqlc, pinned at =0.13.14, following its container iteration order. Retained with both reproducers and their logs under security/generative/findings/pipeline-join-distinct-order/. It matters beyond text churn because the randomised list is the output projection, hence the tuple layout a positional decode reads; in the variant with a trailing select the same instability lands in DISTINCT ON, where argument order must prefix-match ORDER BY and decides which row of each group survives. Row-level divergence against a live database is not yet confirmed. Found while verifying the emitted Python reproducer against the interpreter, in generate(20260911, 9, family=\"pipeline\") and index 12."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-11T15:40:14.021314Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Fixing this inside pgorm is not viable, and that is now established rather than assumed. The nondeterministic order comes from prqlc expanding a star projection, so the obvious pgorm-side fix would be to emit an explicit column list instead of `group this (take 1)`. pgorm cannot: the Pipeline struct carries only bindings, stages, values, reshaped and deduped, and no column knowledge whatsoever. Columns are resolved by prqlc from the source schema, so a pipeline over an entity or a runtime-named source is schema-less on pgorm's side until prqlc resolves it. There is nothing to enumerate. The remaining route is a pinned patch plus an upstream report. A candidate one-line fix exists at prqlc-0.13.14 semantic/lowering.rs, where the lowering of LineageColumn::All iterates LoweredTarget::Input(HashMap<RelationColumn, (CId, usize)>) and then calls sort_by_key on the frame-position usize alone; because the sort is stable over a hash-ordered input, any tie in that key keeps hash order. CId already derives Ord, so widening the key to the pair (frame position, CId) makes the order total at no behavioural cost. That site is a plausible rather than proven origin for this particular failure — but the reproducer committed with this node exits non-zero today, so any candidate patch is empirically verifiable in minutes rather than argued about. prqlc 0.13.14 is the latest release, so there is no upgrade that avoids the work."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-11T15:50:28.646883Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "The fix is now proven, and the earlier candidate was wrong. Patching semantic/lowering.rs to widen input_cols.sort_by_key from the frame-position usize to (position, CId) does NOT fix it: built against the retained reproducer, that change still produced two renderings. The actual site is semantic/resolver/expr.rs in construct_tuple_from_module, which is the star expansion itself. It iterates module.names — a HashMap — sorted only by the declaration order field, so any two declarations sharing an order value have their tie broken by hash iteration, and Rust reseeds RandomState per container, which is why the order moves within a single process. Widening that key to (order, name) makes it total, since names are unique within a module: sorted_by_key(|(n, d)| (d.order, (*n).clone())). With that one line the reproducer reports a single rendering across three fresh processes, and nothing else moves — pgorm's 217 lib unit tests, its 76 pipeline tests and pgorm-query's 77 all pass against the patched compiler, so no existing emitted SQL changes. Validation was done through a scratch [patch.crates-io] against a local copy; the workspace manifest was restored unmodified. The remaining work is to carry this as a real fork with the patch applied, wire it in by [patch.crates-io], and send the same one-liner upstream so the fork can be dropped."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-11T16:18:58.294753Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-23T21:59:13.521228Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Re-verified 2026-09-23 against HEAD 2af59925 (prqlc fork d6f2fa97, carrying b2baa230's total star-expansion order): the retained reproducer security/generative/findings/pipeline-join-distinct-order exits 0 with one rendering across its repeated compilations (SELECT DISTINCT p_id, p_rank, j_id, j_account_id FROM table_1). Its README already records the fix. The node sat in Doing only because completion was never asserted."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-23T21:59:17.451895Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

