        id jiff-temporal
        title "Model PostgreSQL temporal types with jiff instead of chrono"
        kind @feat
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-13T12:03:21.049088Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Operator directed replacing chrono with jiff outright, and doing it before generative.runner rather than after the campaign. postgres-types 0.2.13 already ships with-jiff-0_2, so no fork is needed; it maps civil::Date to DATE, civil::Time to TIME, civil::DateTime to TIMESTAMP and Timestamp to TIMESTAMPTZ. The change is a correction rather than a rename. pgorm currently carries six temporal Value variants, and pgorm-query/src/value.rs renders ChronoDateTimeUtc, ChronoDateTimeLocal and ChronoDateTimeWithTimeZone all to timestamptz — three Rust spellings of one database type, two of which reconstruct an offset PostgreSQL never stored. jiff's Timestamp models the single real thing, so six variants collapse to four and the two that disappear are the fictional ones. That is also why postgres-types has no impl for jiff's Zoned: a timestamptz carries nothing to build a zone from. Dropping DateTime<Local> and DateTime<FixedOffset> is a breaking public API change and is accepted as such. Roughly seventy files are involved: pgorm-query Value/ArrayType/ValueType, pgorm ColumnType, DeriveEntityModel's temporal field detection, pgorm-codegen's DateTimeCrate, pgorm-python's wire snapshot and decode, and forty-three campaign files. The campaign's portable wire format names its temporal kinds datetime, datetime_utc, datetime_local and datetime_fixed after chrono's four shapes, so this is a versioned-format change and not only a rename. Sequencing before the runner means the campaign's oracles and wire format are authored once against jiff instead of being migrated afterwards."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-13T12:11:28.019865Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "A behavioural difference the campaign oracle must model, found by reading postgres-types 0.2.13 src/jiff_02.rs before starting. The ToSql impls for civil::DateTime, Timestamp and civil::Time do not reject sub-microsecond precision: they call Span::round with SpanRound::new().largest(Unit::Microsecond), so a value carrying nanoseconds is ROUNDED to microseconds on write, using jiff's default rounding mode rather than truncation. PostgreSQL stores microseconds, so the lost precision is unavoidable, but the shape of the loss matters. The campaign's current wire format rejects temporal precision Python cannot retain, which is a different contract — under jiff a nanosecond value is accepted and silently changed instead. An oracle expecting equality after a round trip would report a false defect on every such value, and one expecting truncation would report a false defect on any value whose sub-microsecond part rounds up. Whichever way the wire format goes, the reference semantics must expect rounding at the microsecond boundary, and the corpus should carry values either side of a half-microsecond to pin it. Also worth noting the PostgreSQL epoch base is 2000-01-01 rather than the Unix epoch, and both decode and transmit failures flatten to the strings \"value too large to decode\" and \"value too large to transmit\", so a range error carries no detail to attribute it with."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-13T12:13:22.321309Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Correction to the earlier rounding claim: jiff truncates sub-microsecond precision on write. Measured by encoding a civil DateTime through ToSql and reading the wire bytes: .123456, .1234561, .1234565 and .1234569 all give 123456 microseconds, and .999999999 gives 999999. The remainder is discarded whatever its magnitude, including the halfway case and the one that would carry into the next second. The oracle must expect truncation at the microsecond boundary. The contract change still stands, since the campaign wire format rejects precision Python cannot retain while jiff accepts and silently truncates. The corpus needs values with a non-zero sub-microsecond remainder, including one at .999999999, rather than values either side of a half microsecond."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-13T12:13:42.909744Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-13T12:21:37.653392Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Binding and codegen survey findings. The two disappearing kinds are construction-only: pgorm-python/src/results/decode.rs is the whole PostgreSQL to Value mapping and its only timestamptz arm produces ChronoDateTimeUtc, so nothing can ever decode a datetime_local or datetime_fixed. MODEL_KINDS and capabilities.rs already fence them off. Removing them therefore breaks construction, binding, snapshot and equality but never a read path, which de-risks the change considerably: twelve Rust sites, one Python site, six doc and test sites. pgorm-codegen has exactly one type-name selection site, four match arms in src/entity/column.rs, with about twelve further pass-through threading sites, and it takes no chrono, time or jiff dependency at all. Zero golden fixtures need regenerating: no .rs golden in any of the thirteen fixture directories contains a temporal type, and every temporal assertion is inline string matching in five test drivers. Codegen may need no change whatsoever, because gen_import emits only a prelude glob, so Date, Time, DateTime and DateTimeWithTimeZone resolve through src/entity/prelude.rs; repointing those aliases upstream leaves codegen emitting identical text. Mappings that are not one-to-one: check_time rejects chrono leap seconds and jiff cannot represent one at all, so that check goes dead and the leap-second fixture in tests/values.rs must be deleted rather than translated; snapshot Display text changes for naive datetime, space becoming T, and for UTC datetime, trailing UTC becoming Z; aware() collapses to a single outcome and its kind parameter goes dead; codegen/types.py's DateTime generic-wrapper machinery has no jiff analogue, and jiff's bare spellings collide with the prelude aliases in a way the Naive prefixes never did. A user who wrote an offset-carrying value has no offset-preserving replacement and must convert to UTC first. Wire kinds are typed as bare str in every stub with no Literal enumeration, so no stub changes are needed but mypy gives no protection against a stale kind string. Swapping the dependency also changes licenses, DEPENDENCIES.json and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt, so the notices check needs artifact regeneration."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-13T12:23:11.10527Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Two survey claims verified empirically before any code moves. First, jiff's strftime formats leniently: a directive it cannot fill is written verbatim into the output rather than failing. Measured, a civil DateTime formatted with the offset pattern the query builder uses today yields the string 2020-01-01 02:02:02 %:z, with no panic and no error, where the same pattern on a Timestamp correctly yields +00:00 and chrono fails loudly on both. That is a silent SQL-rendering hazard: an offset directive left on the naive path would emit a literal containing %:z, producing invalid SQL rather than a caught error. Every format string reaching a civil type must therefore be offset-free, and a test should pin that the rendered literal never contains a percent sign. Second, jiff rejects the time 24:00:00 where chrono silently wraps it to midnight. The ExactTime codec in the campaign's replay crate exists specifically to catch that wrap, so under jiff the guard becomes redundant at the parse boundary, though rejecting is the better behaviour and the case still deserves a test asserting refusal rather than silent acceptance. Native Display also changes shape: civil DateTime renders with a T separator and Timestamp renders with a trailing Z, where chrono used a space and a trailing UTC respectively, which is exactly the normalisation temporal_text performs."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-13T12:23:24.061725Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Naming resolved, which unblocks the macro inference table and codegen. The hazard the core survey raised is that ColumnType::Timestamp means a naive timestamp while jiff::Timestamp maps to timestamptz, so a field declared with the bare name Timestamp would infer the wrong column type. The resolution is to never expose the name Timestamp in the prelude at all. The four prelude aliases keep their current spellings and are simply repointed: Date becomes jiff::civil::Date, Time becomes jiff::civil::Time, DateTime becomes jiff::civil::DateTime mapping to ColumnType::Timestamp, and DateTimeWithTimeZone becomes jiff::Timestamp mapping to ColumnType::TimestampWithTimeZone. That choice has three consequences worth stating. The macro fast-path table in sql_type_match keys on exactly the identifiers it keys on today, so its entries change meaning without changing spelling, and multi-segment paths and user aliases continue to fall through to the ValueType::column_type fallback and still resolve. pgorm-codegen needs no change whatsoever, because gen_import emits only a prelude glob and the generated identifiers are unchanged, which also means no golden fixture moves. And the six-name Chrono prelude family, which has no in-tree users as a type, is deleted outright rather than renamed. On the campaign's wire format, the rejection contract is kept rather than relaxed: the wire boundary continues to reject sub-microsecond precision instead of accepting a value jiff would silently truncate, so a nanosecond input remains an error at the format boundary and never becomes a library-difference false positive in the oracle. That keeps all three existing rejection points and their tests meaningful and needs no value-format version bump, since the deleted kinds already self-reject as unknown."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-13T13:49:12.514223Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Three findings from the binding port worth keeping. First, the ExactTime guard in the Python binding was deleted rather than retyped, on evidence: jiff's codec builds a time via Time::midnight().checked_add(span), which errors on overflow where chrono's equivalent wrapped, so the driver itself refuses 24:00:00 and the guard could never fire. A dense sweep of 86,402 values plus the boundary cases confirmed every accepted value re-encodes byte-identically. The campaign's replay crate holds its own copy of that guard and the same reasoning is being re-derived there rather than assumed. Second, the jiff pin does not stay free of a timezone database in pgorm-python: pyo3 0.29.2 declares the jiff feature without default-features = false, so unification pulls in jiff-tzdb, jiff-tzdb-platform and jiff-static. Nothing in the binding reads a system zone, but the no-tzdb property cannot be restored without a change in pyo3. That also dragged in defmt-parser, which ships no license file, so its licence texts were taken from its sibling crate and recorded in the supplemental notices. Third, both Python fixture projects had Cargo.lock files with no jiff entry at all, because the earlier phases moved pgorm to jiff without refreshing them, so their --locked gates were already failing before the binding was touched. Regenerating them is part of this change, and it is worth remembering that a detached crate's lock does not follow the workspace."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-14T23:54:19.382561Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

