    {
        id decimal-scale-returned
        title "A returned decimal keeps the scale PostgreSQL gave it"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-14T11:10:51.517016Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The subject returns decimal 0 where the independent reference returns 0.00, both as a scalar and inside a decimal array. Two defects in the 2026-09-14 full profile. The values are numerically equal and differ only in scale. Unlike the other three new classes this one is partly a contract question rather than unambiguously a defect: whether the campaign's decimal comparison should be scale-sensitive is a decision the oracle currently answers yes, and PostgreSQL does carry scale as part of a numeric's identity. Recorded rather than normalized away, because silently widening the comparison would remove the campaign's ability to see scale loss at all. Resolving it means deciding whether pgorm should preserve the scale PostgreSQL returned, or whether the oracle should compare numerically; the first is the stronger guarantee."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T00:03:21.513184Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Not a contract question after all, and the oracle was right to be scale-sensitive. The earlier framing rested on reading the difference as pgorm and the reference disagreeing about whether scale is part of a numeric's identity. The campaign artifacts settle it the other way. In runtime-1283 the same row carries both kinds of field: literal0 reads 0.00 on the subject and 0.00 on the reference, while bound0 reads 0 on the subject and 0.00 on the reference. Scale survives the literal path and is lost on the bound-parameter path, which is the identical signature to bound-datetime-max-precision and not a disagreement about semantics at all. PostgreSQL itself is unambiguous that scale is part of the value: scale(0.00::numeric) is 2, scale(0::numeric) is 0, and the two render as different text. Measured the two paths directly against a live PostgreSQL 16.15 through an example rather than reasoning about them. The read path is clean: every value tested decodes with the scale PostgreSQL reported, as a scalar and inside an array, including 0.00 at scale 2 and 0.000000 at scale 6. The write path loses scale for zero and only for zero. Sent as bound parameters, 0.00 comes back scale 0 and 0.000000 comes back scale 0, while 1.50, 1.5, 12.3400, 0.10 and 100.00 all round-trip their scale exactly. The cause is one line upstream in rust_decimal 1.43.0, src/postgres/common.rs line 70, where to_postgres takes an is_zero shortcut that returns a PostgresDecimal with scale hardcoded to 0 and an empty digit vector, discarding self.scale(); every other path through that function reads self.scale() a few lines below. pgorm reaches it through bind_exact in src/executor/cursor_bind.rs, which hands Value::Decimal straight to rust_decimal's ToSql, so nothing of pgorm's own is wrong, and arrays inherit the same fault because each element re-enters ValueHolder. The chosen fix is to encode zero-with-scale in pgorm's own binding layer rather than fork rust_decimal for one line: the PostgreSQL numeric wire form for zero is fully determined and eight bytes long, ndigits 0, weight 0, sign 0x0000 and dscale set to the value's own scale, with no digit words, and cursor_bind.rs already owns type-aware wire encoding for other values. That also fixes the Python binding for free, since it builds the same Value. Worth noting the asymmetry this closes: the read side already refuses to lose scale, because ExactDecimal in pgorm-python re-encodes what it decoded and errors if the scale differs, so the binding was strict in one direction and silently lossy in the other."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T01:21:22.880525Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

