    {
        id pipeline-native-panic
        title "A generated pipeline compiles to SQL without panicking"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-14T23:59:57.611286Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Filed late: referenced as an existing finding by two other defect nodes but never given a node of its own. The fault is a Rust panic inside prqlc 0.13.14, not inside pgorm and not in the campaign harness. The site is an Option::expect at prqlc/src/sql/gen_expr.rs:687, `name.expect(\"name of this column has not been to be set before generating SQL\")`, reached from Pipeline::into_sql during SQL generation. PyO3 surfaces it as pyo3_runtime.PanicException, a BaseException ordinary handlers cannot swallow, and the campaign executor catches it at executor.py:124 and records class UnexpectedNativePanic with that cause string. Because the panic happens while generating SQL, no SQL is ever emitted, so the item is recorded incomplete rather than defect: the subject produced no observation to compare and the reference side ran normally. Five items exhibit it. One is the originally filed copy at seed 20260911 index 83, whose trace shape is source, from, select, derive, sort, take_range, select, distinct, filter, source, from, select; evidence in security/generative/findings/pipeline-native-panic/, never minimised. Four more regenerated in the 2026-09-14 full profile at seed 20260913 in the pipeline family, all reduced: runtime-3287 is sort, take_range, select, join, distinct, and runtime-3722, runtime-3737 and runtime-4262 are all sort, take_range, select, distinct, select. Each of those four carries a standalone reduced reproducer in both languages, and the Rust one is five pgorm calls, which makes it the strongest attribution lead available. Correction to the acceptance document, which states at security/generative/acceptance/2026-09-14.md that these four retain no panic message and that the attribution is therefore plausible rather than established: all four oracle.json files do carry UnexpectedNativePanic with the exact cause, and they are the only four files among fifteen thousand runtime records containing that string. The attribution is established. Since pgorm already carries a pinned prqlc fork at necessary-nu/prql for the deterministic star expansion fix, a second commit on that fork is the natural route, but the expect must be understood before it is replaced: an unset column name at that point may be a symptom of the same nested-source scope loss that pipeline-hidden-order and nested-source-from-entry exhibit, in which case turning the panic into an error would hide a wrong-SQL defect rather than fix it."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T00:07:54.225063Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Reproduced standalone in Rust with no database, which settles the attribution blocker both this node and pipeline-hidden-order recorded as standalone Rust replay is still required. The reproducer is examples/pipeline_panic_probe.rs, reduced from runtime-3287, and it panics inside into_sql before anything is sent. A backtrace narrows the path considerably and contradicts the natural reading of the panic message: the unset column name is not reached from projection but from ORDER BY generation, translate_select_pipeline at gen_query.rs:196 into translate_column_sort at gen_expr.rs:918 into translate_cid at gen_expr.rs:687. The triggering shape is therefore a relation sorted on a set of columns, a later select that projects some of those columns away, and then an operation forcing a new SELECT scope, a join or a distinct. The retained sort still holds column ids the projection dropped, and at ORDER BY time those ids have no name. PRQL treats sort as a property of a relation that survives a select, so the sort keys become hidden columns that must still be materialised through the CTE boundary for the outer ORDER BY to reference them, and that materialisation is what fails. This makes a single cause plausible for three filed defects rather than one: this panic, pipeline-hidden-order whose emitted SQL carries ORDER BY p_id on a select that never exposed p_id and is rejected 42703, and nested-source-from-entry whose join, sort and take_range shape is rejected 42P01 for a table absent from the FROM clause. All three are the same disagreement between a retained ordering and what an enclosing scope exposes. One caution carried into the fix: the expect must not simply become an error return, because an unset name here is a symptom, and converting the panic would turn the two SQL-rejection defects from loud failures into silently wrong SQL."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T12:26:37.562728Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
