                at 2026-09-15T00:45:01.648858Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found while fixing bound-datetime-max-precision. value_to_string in pgorm-query/src/backend/query_builder.rs renders temporal literals through strftime with the formats year-month-day hour:minute:second and hour:minute:second, neither of which carries a fractional part, so every inlined temporal literal silently drops its sub-second digits. A value of 9999-12-31 23:59:59.999999 is written into SQL as 9999-12-31 23:59:59. This affects Time, DateTime and DateTimeWithTimeZone at all magnitudes, not only at the boundary, and it is the real reason the literal path appeared to succeed where binding failed: the literal path was not preserving the value, it was discarding the part that made binding hard. That makes it more serious than the binding defect it was contrasted against, because a bound parameter that refuses is loud while a literal that truncates is silent. Orthogonal to parameter binding and deliberately left for its own node, because the rendering change moves SQL goldens across the tree including doctests, which nextest does not run, so the doctest suite has to be run separately to see the full blast radius. Worth pairing with the jiff lenient-strftime hazard already recorded on jiff-temporal, that a directive jiff cannot fill is written verbatim into the output rather than failing, so whatever format replaces these must be tested to assert the rendered literal never contains a percent sign."
            }

