    {
        id settled-source-name-resolves
        title "A qualified source name resolves after a settled deduplication"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-15T14:06:30.442793Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "A regression this session introduced and shipped, found by re-running the full generative profile against the fixes. Commit c3f77a50, which made distinct settle the pipeline into its own binding when a take is pending, breaks any later stage that refers to a source by name. The emitted PRQL fails to compile with Unknown name and a hint listing only the binding's internal table_N columns, because a settled relation exposes bare names and nothing else. Thirty-one of the ninety-two findings in the 2026-09-15 full run are this, all in the pipeline family, all recorded as ConstructionError before any SQL is emitted. Bisected rather than guessed: a standalone SQL-only reproducer of item runtime-2387's shape compiles at c3f77a50~1 and fails at c3f77a50 and at every commit since, so the naming work in 39ce5335 neither caused it nor made it worse. The shape is a source nested under a name, a projection renaming its columns, a take_range, a distinct, and then a select whose items are qualified by that source name. The hazard was known when the settle landed: its own commit message records that settling breaks select_sources because source names do not resolve through a CTE, and the implementation guards select_sources specifically. What it does not guard is the general case, an ordinary col(name, column) reference in any later stage, which is the same fault reached through a different door. Two directions are worth weighing rather than one. The narrow repair is to rewrite qualified references to the settled binding the way rebound_sort already repoints sort keys, since naming.rs has the exposed-name map that would need. The broader question is whether distinct should settle at all when a named source is still referenced downstream, which trades the range fix's correctness against this one and probably wants the two conditions separated rather than one flag serving both."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T16:15:17.831066Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T16:16:50.919262Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id outer-ordering-qualifier
        title "An outer ORDER BY names a relation the FROM carries"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-15T14:07:16.27692Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "The part of nested-source-from-entry that the prqlc cid_redirects fix did not reach. That node was closed on its reduced shape, which the fix in 12d01a77 genuinely repairs and which the live regression test alongside it exercises in both directions. The 2026-09-15 full profile then recorded runtime-887 still failing with its original error unchanged, SQLSTATE 42P01 missing FROM-clause entry for table table_4, so the empty redirect map was one cause and not the only one. nplan will not move a Done node back, hence a separate id; the errata on nested-source-from-entry points here. What is known: the full program joins, sorts, takes a range and nests, and its final ORDER BY is qualified by an interior relation that the enclosing FROM does not list, while the reduced form of the same program now compiles and executes. That difference is the lead. The reduction dropped whatever second path also carries a qualifier outward, so the useful next step is to diff the emitted SQL of the full program against the reduced one rather than to re-read the redirect code, and then to shrink the full program again under a predicate pinned to 42P01 so the reduction cannot escape the defect the way the last one did. Worth checking against prqlc's own postprocess::alias_last_sorting and the last-sorting cid-redirect walk, which the upstream work identified as where the damage showed even though the cause sat a layer earlier."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T19:31:33.912638Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Not a distinct defect, and this node should not have been filed. The premise was that the prqlc redirect fix repaired the reduced shape while the full program still failed, so the empty redirect map must have been one cause among several. That premise came from a run whose subject had never linked the fork at all. pgorm-python declares its own workspace, deliberately, to keep Python configuration out of ordinary Rust builds, and cargo reads a crates-io patch table from the workspace root and nowhere else, so the root's prqlc pin never reached it. Its lockfile carried prqlc 0.13.14 from the registry with a checksum, and the campaign builds that extension with maturin under locked, so all three fork commits were invisible to every campaign run this session. Established rather than argued: the exact runtime-887 program was transcribed into a standalone crate, checked byte-identical against the campaign's own generated replay, and compiled twice. With the fork pinned it orders by the in-scope alias and the live server executes it; against registry prqlc it orders by table_4 and fails 42P01 exactly as filed. Deterministic over twenty-five runs. So no prqlc work was needed, the fork was untouched, and nested-source-from-entry was correctly closed the first time — the reopening was wrong and rests on this same mistake. The fix belongs to the pin rather than to the SQL: pgorm-python now carries the same patch table, its lock is refreshed to the git rev, and a build script fails the build when the two tables disagree, which was verified by removing the table and watching cargo silently re-resolve to registry prqlc. The wider lesson is the one campaign-refuses-stale-build already carries and now has a second instance: a campaign that builds its subject from a manifest nobody checks can test something other than what is checked out, and here it did so silently for every run."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-16T23:08:17.205808Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id campaign-evidence-durable
        title "Retained campaign evidence survives an ordinary build clean"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-15T15:35:12.793326Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "The generative campaign writes its run artifacts under target/generative-campaign, and filed defect nodes cite those paths as the evidence behind their claims. cargo clean deletes them. On 2026-09-15 a full disk forced a clean and destroyed the 2026-09-14 full run: fifteen thousand runtime items, thirty-seven finding directories, and the per-item reduced reproducers in both languages. The cost was immediate rather than theoretical. When the next full run reported ninety-two findings against the previous thirty-seven, there was no way to tell which were new, because the baseline no longer existed; the count had to be compared against a handful of numbers that happened to be quoted in the acceptance document and in plan log entries. The curated directories under security/generative/findings are in the repository and survived, but they cover only findings someone thought to promote, and the promotion is manual. Two things are worth separating in the fix. Evidence a node cites has to be durable, which argues for promoting a finding's directory out of target at the moment it is filed rather than leaving the citation pointing into a build directory. Bulk run output can stay disposable, since the campaign is seeded and a run is reproducible in principle, but reproducing a full profile costs hours and only works while the source that produced it is still checked out, so a summary sufficient to diff two runs by finding id and signature should outlive the run itself. A third, smaller point: the campaign's build environment lives under target/generative-build and is also destroyed, which is merely slow to rebuild rather than lossy, but it means a clean leaves the campaign unrunnable until someone works out that it needs maturin 1.15.0 under a 3.14 interpreter."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T17:44:18.473979Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The first run this session whose answer is worth anything, and worth recording what made it so. Three conditions had to hold at once and each had failed separately before. The native bridge was rebuilt so build.json records the revision the tree is actually at, after a run tested a bridge three commits stale and reported a fixed defect as still broken. No agent was touching the database, after a run collided with three of them and returned 2947 FixtureFailure records out of 2972. And a list of the previous run's finding ids was copied out of target beforehand, so the two runs could be diffed by id rather than by remembering numbers. Under those conditions the profile went from ninety-two findings to nine. What that establishes is stronger than the arithmetic: every one of the four fixes confirmed here was confirmed on the original campaign programs rather than on a reduction, which is the distinction that closed nested-source-from-entry too early. The literal reconciliation cleared thirty-nine, the settle repointing thirty, the set-operation bracketing five, and the window frame nine of its ten, the tenth turning out to be the count half of a finding that bundled two discrepancies. The three that remain are each a single filed defect rather than a class. Until this node is fixed, the standing procedure is that a campaign run means nothing unless the bridge revision matches HEAD, nothing else holds the database, and the prior finding list has been preserved somewhere cargo clean cannot reach."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-17T12:42:53.204223Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
