    {
        id quoted-result-name-doubled
        title "A result column reports the name it was given"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-15T16:25:29.587324Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Seven findings, four relational and three pipeline, where the rows and their values match exactly and only a column name differs, always by a doubled double-quote. A column aliased window-quote comes back from the subject as window followed by one quote and from the reference as window followed by two; the same holds for derived49 and for aggregate. Pre-existing rather than caused by this session, since all seven appear in the 2026-09-14 run. PostgreSQL reports a column's actual name rather than its quoted spelling, so a name containing one double quote is reported with one, and doubling is a property of how an identifier is written inside quotes rather than of the identifier itself. That makes the subject almost certainly right and the reference the side that is doubling, most likely by taking the text it emitted into SQL as the name rather than the name it meant to emit, which would be the same class of mistake as the literal renderer that was implementing a stale rule. It should nonetheless be checked in both directions before either side is changed, because the prefixed result-column names the graph uses are bounded to PostgreSQL's sixty-three byte identifier limit through one shared composition, and a doubling applied before that bound would truncate differently from one applied after."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T17:43:47.592835Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Confirmed by a clean full run and narrowed to the pipeline path alone. The 2026-09-15 profile at revision cfa8601f, with the bridge rebuilt to match the tree and the database used by nothing else, reports nine findings against the previous ninety-two, and seven of the nine are this: runtime-1067, runtime-2126, runtime-2201, runtime-2291, runtime-3572, runtime-3671 and runtime-4307, every one differing only in a column name by exactly one lost quote. Diagnosed directly rather than inferred. An alias whose name is the six letters window followed by two quote characters renders through the ordinary query builder as window followed by five quotes inside quotes, which decodes back to the name that was asked for, and through the pipeline as window followed by three, which decodes to a name carrying one quote. So the builder is correct and only the pipeline path loses a quote, which places the fault in how the name reaches or leaves prqlc rather than in pgorm's own identifier quoting, whose doubling is a plain replace of each quote by two. Checked for the worse reading and it is not there: aliases crafted to break out of a quoted identifier, one closing the quote and opening a select, another closing and appending a statement terminator, render identically and correctly on both paths, so this is a lost character rather than an injection. That also explains why the sqlmap campaign, which carries quoted-identifier controls, has never reported it. The same finding is already retained as pipeline-quoted-output from 2026-09-11, filed then as two authored quotes becoming one output quote and never given a node, and its README records that minimization and a standalone Rust replay were still required; the replay now exists in the probe described here."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-16T23:10:00.443414Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Root cause found, and it is upstream in prqlc with no pgorm involvement at all. Reproduced from plain PRQL source text, which removes the builder, the adapter and the whole AST path from the question: compiling a select that aliases a column to the three characters a, quote, b and compiling one that aliases it to a, quote, quote, b both emit the identical SQL, id AS quote a quote quote b quote. Two different names, one output, so the mapping from name to emitted identifier is not injective and one of the two names cannot be asked for. The direction is clearer from pgorm's side than the earlier reading suggested: it is not that a quote is lost in transit, it is that the emitter appears to un-escape an already-doubled pair before re-doubling, so a name carrying two quote characters collapses to one while a name carrying one survives. That also explains why the ordinary query builder is unaffected, since its quoted method does a plain replace of each quote by two and never inspects what it is given. Confirms the earlier probe's conclusion that the fault sits at the prqlc boundary rather than in pgorm's quoting, and narrows it from the boundary to the emitter. Still not an injection: aliases crafted to close the quoted identifier and open a new clause render correctly on both paths, because the failure is a collapse of two characters into one rather than a failure to escape. The fix belongs on the necessary-nu fork alongside the three commits already there, which is also where it can be tested against prqlc's own snapshot suite rather than only through pgorm."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-16T23:33:06.286181Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
