    {
        id count-of-expression
        title "Counting an expression skips its nulls"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-15T17:26:47.262751Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "The second half of the window-semantics finding, which bundles two unrelated discrepancies; the frame half is fixed and this one is not. Counting an expression emits COUNT star rather than COUNT of that expression, so a null row is counted where SQL says it should be skipped. The two forms differ exactly when the counted expression is null for some row, which makes this quiet rather than loud: a column with no nulls gives the same answer either way, and the defect only appears once a null reaches the aggregate. That is also why it is worth fixing rather than documenting, since a count that silently includes nulls is the kind of wrong answer a caller has no reason to check. Plausibly accounts for some of the ten findings originally grouped under the window frame heading, a two-against-one difference among them being the obvious candidate, so the count should be re-derived from a clean run rather than assumed once the frame fix is confirmed. The pipeline already distinguishes the two spellings at its own surface, since count_rows and count are separate functions, so the loss is in the translation rather than in what the caller can express. Worth checking count_distinct in the same pass, which has the same shape and the same opportunity to collapse to a star."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-15T19:31:58.483388Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

