    {
        id pipeline-rejects-backtick-name
        title "A pipeline refuses a name it cannot spell in PRQL"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-16T23:29:13.973394Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found while fixing the quoted-name collapse upstream, and more serious than the defect it was found beside. PRQL quotes an identifier with backticks and its lexer has no escape for its own quoting character, so a name containing a backtick cannot be written in PRQL at all: the body of a backtick-quoted ident is taken verbatim, and a doubled backtick lexes as two adjacent idents rather than as one escaped character. Usually that is a hard parse error, which would be tolerable. Not always. Asking for a column whose name is min, backtick, salary compiles silently to a select of MIN of salary, identical to what an ordinary aggregate call would produce, so a name has become a function call and the query means something the caller did not ask for. That is injection through an identifier rather than through a value, and it matters directly for pgorm because the pipeline mints PRQL text from Rust-side identifiers that a caller supplies: Alias::new takes any string. The upstream compiler is the wrong place to fix it. Giving the surface grammar an escape is a language change rather than a bug fix, it would alter the meaning of inputs that compile today, and it is not pgorm's to make; the fork carries a test pinning the current behaviour so the decision is recorded rather than assumed. The fix belongs here: a pipeline should refuse a name it cannot spell, which means rejecting a backtick at the boundary where a name enters the PRQL text, with an error naming the character and the identifier. Worth checking the whole surface for the same hazard while doing it, since the sqlmap campaign's quoted-identifier controls test double quotes and have no backtick case at all, and the generative corpus reaches this only by accident."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-16T23:30:39.186277Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Correction to this node's own filing, made before any work was done on it: pgorm is not exposed, and the premise that its pipeline mints PRQL text from caller identifiers is wrong. Tested rather than reasoned. Four names were pushed through a pipeline against the fixed fork, including the min-backtick-salary case that subverts the PRQL surface and a payload crafted to close the backtick quote and append further pipeline stages. All four render as ordinary quoted SQL identifiers and none changes the query's meaning: the crafted payload comes out as a single quoted column name containing spaces, pipes and braces. The reason is structural rather than lucky. pgorm builds prqlc's PL AST directly and hands it to pl_to_rq, so the lexer that cannot escape a backtick is never in the path, and an identifier travels as a Rust string in an AST node rather than as text to be re-parsed. The defect is therefore real in PRQL the language and absent from pgorm the library, and the node should be closed rather than fixed. Two things worth keeping from it. The fork carries a test pinning the current lexer behaviour so the hazard is recorded for anyone who later compiles PRQL source text through prqlc::compile rather than through the AST, which pgorm does in exactly one place, a test-only helper. And the sqlmap campaign's identifier controls cover double quotes with no backtick case at all, which is worth adding not because pgorm is vulnerable but because the control would prove it stays that way."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-16T23:33:44.99552Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id coverage-obligations-unreached
        title "The generator reaches every obligation the matrix declares"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-16T23:54:31.726124Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "The full profile's remaining failure, and the only one left once every defect cleared. The run of 2026-09-17 records twenty thousand construction programs, five thousand live-database programs, five hundred invalid inputs and twenty-two controls, all passing with zero defects and zero incompletes, and still fails, because a hundred and seventy-three declared coverage obligations were never reached. That is a different claim from a defect: it says the campaign did not exercise everything its own matrix promised, so the silence over those cells means nothing rather than meaning clean. Pre-existing and stable rather than newly introduced, which the kept summary from the previous run establishes: the same hundred and seventy-three were outstanding there, and that run carried both coverage-unsatisfied and unexpected-verdict where this one carries only the first. So the fixes removed the defects and left the gap exactly as it was. The gap is broad rather than concentrated, which argues against one missing generator rule: twenty-seven obligations under value, twenty under pipeline, eighteen under graph, seventeen under crud, fourteen under types, ten each under entities, schema and sequences, nine each under names and patterns, and eight each under array and conditions. Several of the array cells name u64 and vector element types, which suggests some obligations may be declared for shapes the generator cannot currently construct at all rather than merely fails to reach by chance, and those two cases want different answers: a generator that cannot reach a cell needs a rule, while an obligation declared for a shape the library does not support should be retired from the matrix with that reason recorded. Establishing which of the two each cell is, before writing any generator code, is the first piece of work."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-17T13:15:00.938568Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The filing's framing was wrong for most of the list, and the shape of the evidence said so before any code was read. A hundred and thirty-two of the hundred and seventy-three were family-variation cells and not one of them had ever been satisfied, which a twenty-thousand-program run does not achieve by chance across eleven independent families. The producer of coverage tokens emitted four token shapes and none of them could ever be a family-dot-variation token, so those obligations were declared in the matrix without the attribution that discharges them. They were generated all along and coverage could not see them. A second, smaller instance of the same thing: registered sources sat at zero of six while entities and graphs were complete, because construction reported a registration only for the two node kinds that carry a name and a sources list resolves to a value that carries none. So the breadth argued for one cause rather than twelve, which is the opposite of what the filing assumed. Only three cells were genuinely impossible, all decode cells and each one already declared so by the matrix's own result-kind field: an unsigned sixty-four bit decode, which PostgreSQL has no type for and the decoder never tags; a vector decode, which the pinned image cannot produce since it has no pgvector; and a char decode, whose row declares itself text because a Rust char travels in a text column. The array cells this node flagged as the likely impossible ones are not impossible at all, which was checked rather than assumed: the array type carries both tags, the wire layer takes the element type from the inferred type without a refusing arm, and the only refusal on that path is nested arrays. They stay in the matrix as generator gaps. Measured rather than estimated, the outstanding count went from a hundred and seventy-three to twenty-two, and a hundred and thirty-eight of the hundred and fifty-one closed needed no generator change whatsoever."
            }

