    {
        id prqlc-wildcard-distinct-key
        title "prqlc keeps a wildcard out of an unqualified DISTINCT ON key"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-17T00:37:16.543005Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "The upstream half of distinct-star-projection, which pgorm now works around correctly but does not cause. prqlc lowers a group-take-one to a Take with a partition and then chooses between SELECT DISTINCT and DISTINCT ON by comparing the partition against the columns in frame; over a relation still projecting a wildcard those sets match while nothing else is fused into the query, and diverge as soon as a later stage is, at which point the wildcard itself lands in the DISTINCT ON key list. Whether that is legal turns on one line in gen_query, which omits the table prefix when the query holds exactly one table: with two or more relations the star is written qualified and PostgreSQL accepts it as a whole-row composite, and with one it is written bare and the grammar rejects it with syntax error at or near star, SQLSTATE 42601. So prqlc emits SQL outside the grammar it targets, in a case its own prefix rule creates. Two shapes of fix are available upstream and the choice is not obvious: qualify the star in a DISTINCT ON key regardless of the table count, which is minimal but makes the prefix rule conditional on context rather than on arity, or decline to choose DistinctOn at all when the partition holds a wildcard and fall back to the form that is already correct. The second is closer to the real invariant, that a wildcard is not a key expression. pgorm's own hoist settles the relation into a binding before the next stage can fuse, which is a correct workaround needing no pin change, so this is not urgent; it belongs on the necessary-nu fork beside the four commits already there, and it should carry a test in prqlc's own suite since the pgorm-side regression cannot see the multi-relation case that is already correct."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-17T12:59:29.508816Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Both halves of this node's filing were wrong, and the live server settled it rather than argument. The filing preferred declining DistinctOn when the partition holds a wildcard, on the reasoning that a wildcard is not a key expression. It is one: PostgreSQL accepts a qualified whole-row reference as a key and treats it as exactly equivalent to deduplicating whole rows, checked over a table with duplicates and again over an all-NULL left-join side. And the fallback that option implies would have changed the answer rather than only the spelling. Over four rows with a duplicate, deduplicating whole rows and then projecting returns three where SELECT DISTINCT over the projection returns two, and the qualified whole-row key returns three, so falling back would have silently given the wrong count. Worse, the fallback does not even reach SELECT DISTINCT: gating that branch lands in the row-number filter instead, which emits PARTITION BY star and fails with the identical syntax error, which the sqlite, mysql and glaredb targets already demonstrate today. So the qualifying option is right, and the objection to it is weaker than it reads, since the identifier translation was already conditional on context and prefix elision is cosmetic rather than semantic. The defect is also wider than filed. A bare star reaches three positions from plain PRQL, not one: the DISTINCT ON list, a window PARTITION BY, and GROUP BY. The first two are fixed by qualifying. GROUP BY is deliberately left broken and commented, because qualifying there only exchanges one error for another: PostgreSQL will not propagate a functional dependency from a whole-row var to its columns, so the query fails 42803 where it previously failed 42601, and the real answer is wildcard expansion, which is the one thing a wildcard says cannot be done."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-17T12:59:53.173083Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-17T13:00:20.568828Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id prqlc-group-by-wildcard
        title "Grouping by a whole relation names its columns"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-17T12:59:45.4974Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "The third position a bare star reaches in prqlc's output, left unfixed when the other two were repaired, and left with a comment in the source naming why. Grouping a star relation and aggregating emits GROUP BY star, which PostgreSQL rejects with SQLSTATE 42601, and qualifying the star the way the DISTINCT ON and PARTITION BY positions now do only exchanges one error for another: PostgreSQL will not propagate a functional dependency from a whole-row variable to the individual columns a projection selects, so the qualified form fails 42803 instead. That is why it was excluded rather than swept in. The real fix is to expand the wildcard into the relation's actual columns at that point, which is the one thing a wildcard means the compiler does not yet know; it therefore needs the column list to be resolved before lowering rather than a change at the rendering seam where the other two were fixed. Not urgent for pgorm: the pipeline settles a star relation into its own binding before any stage can fuse into a group, so the shape does not arise through the builder, and the campaign's generator does not construct it either. Reachable from plain PRQL source, so it matters to anyone compiling PRQL text through the fork, and it is pinned by a test in prqlc's own suite rather than left to be rediscovered."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T14:11:38.700517Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found 2026-09-24 by the re-pin agent while probing fork 4ff45819 (dd9a0fee's fix) through the prql! macro's compile options. dd9a0fee refused a grouping by an unknown relation's star only when the star itself was selected, so a named column of that relation read beside the whole-row key slipped through: `from tally | group this (aggregate {n = count this}) | select {fruit, n}` compiled to `SELECT fruit, COUNT(*) AS n FROM tally GROUP BY tally.*`, and PostgreSQL 16 rejected it at execution with 42803 (\"column tally.fruit must appear in the GROUP BY clause\"). The same held for a HAVING filter on such a column (`... | filter fruit == \"apple\"`) and for a sort on one that pushed the grouping into a CTE. Under d6f2fa97 the shape emitted `GROUP BY *`, which the prql! oracle refused at build time, so for prql! users dd9a0fee alone would have moved it from a build-time refusal to a run-time failure. Fixed in the fork at 2ce6a242, before the re-pin landed: a named column the SELECT reads is written out as a key beside the star (`GROUP BY fruit, tally.*`), and a read of the star itself is still refused at compile time (\"does not support grouping by all columns of a relation whose columns are unknown while using its columns outside an aggregation\"). pgorm re-pins straight to 2ce6a242, so no pgorm commit ever carried the 42803 shape."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T14:38:45.493273Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id coverage-remaining-gaps
        title "The last twenty-two obligations are reached or retired"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-17T13:15:14.501679Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "What survives the attribution fix, all of it a genuine generator gap rather than a reporting one, and in three groups that want different work. Five decode cells for decimal, json, enum, ipnetwork and mac address need a checked codec to survive the round trip, and the replay crate keeps those codecs private; reading them as their bare Rust type would silently change the value, which is exactly the class of defect this campaign exists to catch, so they are deliberately excluded rather than closed cheaply. Closing them means exporting the codecs. Fourteen cells for unsigned sixty-four bit and vector values and their arrays are constructible, which was verified against the type layer rather than assumed, but the grammar filters them out, and the obvious route is closed for a reason worth recording: construction-only programs contribute no coverage tokens at all, and the inspection oracle explicitly refuses a program holding an explicit unsigned value with an uncovered-semantics error. The remaining route is the rejection family, which matches the matrix policy's own construction-or-rejection wording. Three family cells are simply ungenerated: an empty insert batch, whose documented behaviour of reporting zero rather than writing is untested by the campaign despite being a stated contract; an omitted write; and entity hooks, since no active-model behaviour hook is exercised anywhere in the corpus. The empty batch is the one worth doing first, because the contract is written down and a campaign that never exercises it cannot notice the contract breaking."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T15:04:46.787283Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Closed on merged main, 2026-09-24. The authoritative full run full-4311ca278c80 at 2377cb16 (clean tree, after the prqlc re-pin to fork 2ce6a242, the tokenizer backslash fix and the six coverage commits shipped from this node's worktree) passed outright: 20000/20000 construction, 5000/5000 live, 500/500 invalid expected-rejection, 22/22 controls, compile suite pass (72 cases), coverage 448/448 declared with 0 outstanding, zero findings. The 22 obligations closed rather than retired: the five result-decode cells through replay codecs made public and read as checked codecs (f8d122ed); the fourteen u64/vector value and array cells through the rejection family — u64 beyond i64 refused at serialisation, vector refused as a missing type (3513cce2); the empty batch as a rejection rule (Python's Insert refuses an empty row set; Rust's Insert::many(empty) is not reachable from the Python surface), the omitted write through sequence inserts, and entities.hooks by attribution only, the before_save hook having been exercised all along (6a7a7fb2). The baseline run on this branch also surfaced that pgorm's deliberate UnquotableIdentifier refusal (f48c6425) was unmodelled by the reference; the campaign now checks the refusal instead of tripping on it (c72c3a9e). One correction to this node's own commit history: 84bb4dcf and 2377cb16 attribute the fixture-admin timeouts they fix to a loaded host, but the operator's laptop was sleeping during those runs, which pauses the Docker VM and jumps the wall clock past a two-second statement limit — the likelier cause. The fixes stand regardless, since they bound only administrative sessions and leave generated programs under their two-second limit; this run, on an awake and otherwise idle machine, had no timeout of either kind."
            }

