    {
        id prqlc-pin-incomplete
        title "Every detached workspace resolves the pinned prqlc, discovered not listed"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-17T13:28:34.570857Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Three manifests pin the prqlc fork; twelve are eligible. The eight that do not — security/sqlmap/adapter, security/generative/replay, pgorm-python/tests/application-binding, pgorm-python/tests/codegen-entities, and the five security/generative/findings reproducers — each declare their own [workspace], depend on pgorm by path, and so resolve prqlc 0.13.14 from crates.io. Verified in their committed lockfiles: source = \"registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index\", checksum 43a839cc.\n\nThe defect is in the guard I wrote, not only in the manifests. tests/prqlc_pin_tests.rs line 70 is `const DETACHED: [&str; 2] = [\"pgorm-python\", \"security/generative/bridge\"]` — a hand-written list of the two cases I already knew about. It reads like a guard against the class and is an assertion about two instances, so every detached workspace added since has been unprotected by construction and the test stayed green throughout. The fix is to discover the set rather than list it: walk the tree for Cargo.toml files declaring [workspace] that depend on pgorm or pgorm-codegen by path, and require the patch table of each. Then the next detached crate is covered on the day it is created.\n\nTwo of the eight matter beyond hygiene. security/sqlmap/adapter is the subject of the SQL-injection campaign, so the binary that campaign scans is not the binary that ships; the fork carries 9023adad, which changes how identifiers are quoted, and identifier handling is the one surface where injection in an ORM can actually live. security/generative/replay reproduces campaign findings while the bridge that generates them is pinned, so the generator and the replayer are two different compilers and a replay can diverge from its finding for reasons that have nothing to do with the defect.\n\nOn severity, stated carefully because I overstated it first. 9023adad is not a quote breakout in its main case. sqlparser's escape_quoted_string passes an already-doubled delimiter through and doubles a lone one, so `a\"b` and `a\"\"b` both render `\"a\"\"b\"`: the mapping is not injective and a name silently loses a character. That is a correctness defect, not an escape — a caller cannot leave the quoted context that way. The backslash case is the one worth testing rather than asserting, since escape_quoted_string also passes through a delimiter preceded by a backslash and PostgreSQL gives backslash no meaning inside a quoted identifier. Reaching any of it needs pipeline::named_runtime, the deliberate runtime-name entry point; the ordinary alias path is AliasName(&'static str) and cannot carry runtime data at all."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-21T18:22:22.817775Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id supply-chain-unscanned
        title "The dependency tree is scanned for advisories on a schedule"
        kind @ci
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-17T16:12:48.147713Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "The repository had no way to learn that a dependency had acquired a published advisory. No cargo-deny or cargo-audit config, no Dependabot, no SBOM, and no CI job running any of them; a grep for OWASP, ASVS, CWE, CVE and \"threat model\" returns nothing anywhere in the tree. The first notice of a vulnerability would have been someone happening to read a mailing list.\n\nMeasured before writing policy. cargo-deny was already installed, and over the tree as it stands: advisories ok, bans ok, sources ok. So there is nothing live to fix — the gap was never knowing, and never being able to know tomorrow.\n\nThe license allowlist in deny.toml is derived from `cargo deny list` rather than copied from a template, so every entry is a license already present and read. r-efi is the only crate offering a copyleft option and is licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0 OR LGPL-2.1-or-later, so omitting LGPL selects a permissive alternative rather than excluding the crate. The prqlc fork is named under sources.allow-git, which is what makes it a reviewed exception instead of an unremarked git dependency: a second fork now has to be a deliberate edit to that file.\n\nThe schedule is the substance of the CI workflow, not the pull-request trigger. A tree does not have to change to become vulnerable, so a check that only ran on PRs would stay green through exactly the case it exists to catch. The run uploads the resolved lockfile because the root Cargo.lock is untracked; without it a green run reports on a tree nobody can reconstruct afterwards, which is how the campaign lost its baseline.\n\nOne finding is deliberately reported rather than fixed. cargo-deny flags four wildcard dependencies — pgorm-pool and three siblings referred to by path with no version. My first read was that intra-workspace paths are exempt and this was a false positive; allow-wildcard-paths does not cover them, because none of pgorm, pgorm-pool, pgorm-query, pgorm-macros or pgorm-sql-macro sets publish = false and crates.io rejects a path dependency carrying no version. So `cargo publish` would fail on these today. That is real, but it is a defect in releasing rather than in supply chain, and the two fixes — a version alongside each path, or an explicit publish = false — are opposite claims about whether this fork is distributed. Settling that by config edit would bury the question, so wildcards is set to warn and the choice stays with the operator."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-21T18:22:29.034891Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id sqlmap-smoke-unrunnable
        title "The smoke profile schedules only cases its own scan strength can detect"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-17T16:23:05.858085Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "The smoke profile cannot pass. It schedules three cases at level 2 / risk 1, and one of them — parameters — has a control that only sqlmap's level 3 boundaries can reach, so the run ends with \"Undetected controls: parameters-B\" every time. Measured both ways rather than argued: at smoke's settings the control completes with 95 requests and zero findings while select and pipeline-literal each detect in 36, and the same case scanned at the full profile's level 3 / risk 2 passes on B, E, U, S and T with the control firing and no protected finding. So pgorm has not regressed and the oracle is not broken; the profile asks for a detection its own scan strength cannot produce.\n\nTwo details worth keeping. The failure is not silent at the database: the control 500s on 42 of its requests, and the payloads that reach it produce `invalid input syntax for type boolean: \"alice\"` and `unterminated quoted identifier`, which is the injection landing and then erroring identically for the true and the false variant — no observable difference, so boolean-based blind has nothing to compare. And neither the control nor `q::inject_parameters` has changed since the acceptance run: the two commits touching the adapter's main.rs on 2026-09-14 left the parameters arm alone, and inject_parameters has no history since 2026-09-10.\n\nThe reason nobody noticed is separate and worse. The sqlmap workflow has run exactly once in CI, the scheduled full profile on 2026-09-14, and it never scanned anything: it died in setup with `error: cannot update the lock file security/sqlmap/adapter/Cargo.lock because --locked was passed to prevent this`, published `sqlmap / full: NOT-RUN`, and exited. The smoke profile has never run in CI at all. So a profile that fails by construction has sat behind a job that has never reached the scanner, and the local acceptance run on 2026-09-14 was full, which passes. That setup failure is now incidentally repaired — regenerating the adapter's lockfile under prqlc-pin-incomplete made `cargo build --locked` succeed, verified here — so the next scheduled run will actually scan, and will fail on smoke for the reason above unless the profile is fixed first.\n\nThe fix is a choice, not a repair: raise smoke to level 3 / risk 2 and pay the time on every pull request, or drop parameters from smoke and keep the fast check honest about covering less. Swapping in a case whose control fires at level 2 is the third option and probably the right one, but which case that is needs measuring rather than picking."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T16:21:33.808352Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Resolved by swapping the smoke case whose control could not fire at smoke strength. The decision was to replace `parameters` with a case whose control fires at level 2 / risk 1, keeping smoke fast and B-only; the replacement was measured, not picked. Every one of the 24 cases carrying a scheduled B technique was scanned at smoke strength (level 2, risk 1, technique B) twice, into target/sqlmap-ci/measure-a and measure-b. All 23 non-`parameters` cases detected their control on both runs; `parameters` completed 95 requests with no finding on both, confirming it is unreachable there rather than flaky. Choice among the reliable candidates by coverage: `select` and `pipeline-literal`, the two cases smoke already carried, are both single-quoted literals in a WHERE. `enum` (`SELECT name FROM reviews WHERE status = CAST('ready' AS fixture.\"{input}\")`) is the only B-scheduled case whose control is a double-quoted identifier, so it adds identifier-quoting coverage the two literal cases lack, exercising the `Name::runtime` / schema-qualified `TypeName` path the pgorm-query restructuring rewrote. Its control fires through `\")` plus a commenting subquery payload at level 2, 64 control requests, ~2 s. Committed as 1e2a6118; profiles.json smoke.cases is now [select, pipeline-literal, enum], and the runner test pins the list and records why `parameters` cannot be on it. Two smoke runs against the fixed tree, target/sqlmap-ci/smoke-fix-1 and smoke-fix-2, both reported `smoke: PASS`, 6 of 6 scans complete (3 cases x control+protected), no undetected controls, no protected findings, clean cleanup, source unchanged. The full-profile re-check runs separately as extra assurance over the pgorm-query re-founding and the prqlc re-pin; it is not this node's acceptance, which is that smoke schedules only cases its own scan strength can detect."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T16:21:33.966967Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id pipeline-identifier-quoting-own
        title "pgorm quotes its own pipeline identifiers instead of trusting a patched dependency"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-18T16:56:06.326904Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "A security property is living in a [patch.crates-io] table, and patch tables do not reach consumers. Root Cargo.toml line 60 declares prqlc as an ordinary registry dependency at =0.13.14; lines 113-114 patch it to the necessary-nu fork. Cargo honours [patch] only from the root of the workspace being built, so a crate that adds pgorm to its own manifest resolves prqlc 0.13.14 from crates.io. That is not a deduction — the nine detached manifests fixed under prqlc-pin-incomplete each depended on pgorm by path and each resolved registry prqlc, which is the same relationship a downstream consumer has.\n\nThe pipeline hands identifiers to prqlc unquoted rather than quoting them itself: src/pipeline/expr.rs:55 calls Iden::to_string, the unquoted spelling, bypassing pgorm-query's own Iden::prepare, and src/pipeline/builder.rs:463 and src/pipeline/sources.rs:433 do the same for from_schema and named_runtime. So quoting is entirely prqlc's, and which prqlc decides whether it is safe.\n\nVerified rather than argued, in three steps. Registry prqlc 0.13.14 gen_expr.rs:963 passes the raw ident to sql_ast::Ident::with_quote. sqlparser 0.62 escape_quoted_string then skips a delimiter preceded by a backslash — its own source comment reads \"the quote is already escaped with a backslash, skip\" — so a name containing \\\" renders with the backslash-quote intact. PostgreSQL gives backslash no meaning inside a quoted identifier, so the name ends there: against a live PostgreSQL 16, SELECT \"a\\\" FROM injtest.secret --\" FROM injtest.items reports column \"a\\\" does not exist, the identifier having terminated at the backslash-quote with the remainder parsed as SQL. Exploitation follows in an alias position, where the leading identifier need not resolve: SELECT 'alice' AS \"x\\\" , (SELECT password FROM injtest.secret) AS \"leak\" returns alice alongside hunter2. That is data exfiltration through a column name.\n\nCaller data reaches it in the shipping Python binding, which builds these identifiers from Python strings — pgorm-python/src/pipeline/recipe.rs:106 maps Column(table, column) to pl::col(Alias::new(table), Alias::new(column)), recipe.rs:126 routes Named through as_runtime, and slots.rs:69 and :90 pass caller qualifiers into named_runtime. pgorm-python itself carries the patch and has a build.rs drift check, so that binding as built here is safe; the exposure is a Rust consumer taking pgorm as a dependency and passing untrusted text into col, named_runtime or from_schema.\n\nThe sqlmap suite cannot find this and its passing is not evidence against it. The pipeline-projection case drives precisely the vulnerable API at security/sqlmap/adapter/src/main.rs:100, but the harness builds inside this workspace where the patch applies, so it only ever exercises the safe renderer; and even unpatched it would miss, because the level-3 double-quote boundaries are \", \") and \")) with no backslash among them, while a bare \" is correctly doubled even upstream. The one payload shape that defeats the escaper is outside the boundary catalogue.\n\nThe fix is not to pin harder. Pinning is what failed: a consumer cannot be made to inherit our patch. pgorm should quote or validate pipeline identifiers before they reach prqlc — reject an embedded quote and NUL, bound to 63 bytes, or pre-double delimiters as fork commit 9023adad does inside prqlc — so the property holds whichever prqlc resolves. Keep the fork pin for the determinism fixes it also carries, but stop it being what stands between a caller's string and a quoted identifier. The generative campaign already recorded a related sighting in security/generative/findings/pipeline-quoted-output, where a column named derived24\"\" came back as derived24\", the same injectivity loss from the other direction; that finding should be revisited against this."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-19T21:59:49.607698Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Gated at `Pipeline::into_sql` rather than at the four constructors, on the `ReservedAlias` pattern already there. The choke point is the whole argument: `adapter::compile` has exactly one call site (terminal.rs:53), so every identifier the pipeline can hand prqlc passes the screen, including ones a stage written after this change introduces. Validating `col`, `from_schema`, `named_runtime` and `as_runtime` would have covered today's four and nothing else.\n\nThe predicate is `name.contains('\"') || name.contains('\\0')` and nothing more. Escaping was rejected on the evidence rather than on taste: the pinned fork's `quoted_ident` (9023ada, prqlc/prqlc/src/sql/gen_expr.rs:1004) already doubles every delimiter before handing it to sqlparser, so a pgorm that pre-doubled would emit a name doubled twice under the fork and correctly once under the registry crate — the same version-dependence, relocated. Refusing is the only answer that reads the same under both. No length bound was added: an identifier past 63 bytes is a collision-after-truncation question, not a representability one, and conflating them would have made this rule answer two things badly.\n\nThe walker is the substance. `collect_aliases` saw alias positions only, and it was also the walker that forgot `SString` — so an alias inside an expression `window::written_aggregates` assembled was never screened for reserved names either. Both now go through one immutable `walk` beside `walk_mut`, which additionally visits a call's `name` (a rewrite must not repoint a stage's verb; a screen must see every name that leaves) and covers `FString` alongside `SString` so a constructor added later is screened before it is written. What the walk cannot reach is accounted for: `Piece::Text` inside an assembled expression is SQL pgorm wrote itself, and the `let table_N` / `main` / `Project` names in `adapter::stmt` are minted internally and never carry caller text.\n\nEight tests, including the live-verified payload `x\\\" , (SELECT password FROM secret) AS \"leak` through each of the four constructors. `identifier_refusal_precedes_the_prqlc_call` is the one that pins the property a single build cannot demonstrate by linking both compilers: it carries the payload on a pipeline whose `append` also mismatches its column counts — which every prqlc rejects as `Compile` — so the identifier error coming back at all is only possible if the screen returned before the compiler ran. `a_backslash_without_a_quote_still_renders` pins the other edge, that a backslash alone is not refused.\n\nOne consequence for a neighbouring node: the generative finding at security/generative/findings/pipeline-quoted-output is the same injectivity loss seen from the output side — an authored `derived24\"\"` returning as `derived24\"`. That name is now refused at `into_sql`, so the discrepancy it records is no longer reachable through the pipeline. Its replay command still exits 1, but for a different reason than the one the README describes, and the finding wants revisiting rather than closing on this commit's word."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-21T18:22:06.162426Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

