            {
                id query-raw-relation
                title "A raw SQL fragment can stand in relation position"
                kind @feat
                deps (query-raw-template-words)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-19T23:12:31.604424Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "FromItem (types.rs:393-402) offers Table/SubQuery/ValuesList/FunctionCall — no raw variant, so a foreign SQL fragment cannot enter a built query as a relation. Expression position has Expr::cust; relation position has nothing. The crate's own executor paid for this twice: wrap_raw_select (src/executor/paginator.rs:395-402) and the count path (:129-133) hand-format \"SELECT * FROM ({select}) AS ...\" around a caller's raw statement, then need a pg_query-based marker census (:411-450) to make the interpolation safe. The fix is a validated raw relation riding the existing template machinery (single-SELECT check, $N renumbering — exactly what the paginator already hand-rolls), after which wrap_raw_select is deleted and the paginator becomes an ordinary builder consumer. Naming follows query-raw-template-words, hence the dep."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-20T17:01:55.523093Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-20T17:49:32.555363Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Landed as a template-class relation, not a raw one, per the 10a04f40 vocabulary: FromItem::Template(SqlTemplate, Name) reached through SqlTemplate::from_sql(sql, values) — a second constructor on the existing type that reads the $ grammar of REAL SQL (Grammar::Sql: only $N is a marker, $$ opens a dollar-quoted body) rather than SqlTemplate::new's authored-template grammar. No new exported item and so no sql.surface change: the grammar is a reading, not a property of the value, and both constructors hand back the same resolved segment sequence, so a second validated-template type would have doubled the surface for no new invariant. No `from_template` builder method either — the Result is SqlTemplate's, one step before the chain, and `.from()` already takes IntoFromItem. No &'static str `from_raw` sibling: relation-position fragments come from callers' runtime statements, which is exactly what the template class exists for. Renumbering is solved IN THE GENERAL CASE and for free: each resolved value re-enters through prepare_simple_expr, so push_param renumbers the fragment's markers into the enclosing statement's space in first-reference order (a value read twice becomes two parameters; markers read out of order come out in order with the values permuted). Tested with a fragment embedded in an outer statement that binds values before and after it. Diagnosis correction for the record: the filing note said the paginator's pg_query marker census was needed because the sql.token tokenizer cannot see comments or dollar quoting, and exec.paginator.raw+3's body said the same, citing sql.token.limits+2 — but that rule now says the OPPOSITE (comments lex as one Space token; dollar-quoted and E'…' bodies as one Quoted token). The tokenizer was upgraded and paginate.md was never revisited, so the rule contradicted its own citation; that stale premise is what made the hand-formatting look unavoidable. The census is now the template machinery's and is strictly stronger than check_markers was — it also refuses a supplied value the statement never reads. What stayed in the ORM is the libpg_query precondition (single row-returning SELECT, taken at the parser's extent), because pg_query is the ORM's dependency and deliberately not the query builder's."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-20T17:54:31.968723Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id query-identity-columns
                title "Identity columns are what the word Identity means"
                kind @feat
                deps (query-name-vocabulary)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-19T23:12:35.006019Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "AutoIncrement renders the legacy serial family only (backend/query_builder.rs:1430-1434); GENERATED { ALWAYS | BY DEFAULT } AS IDENTITY — PostgreSQL's recommended replacement since 10 — is inexpressible, and codegen consequently cannot round-trip a modern schema faithfully. Deliberately blocked on query-name-vocabulary: that rename moves pgorm's key-column Identity type to Key, freeing the word so the type that expresses GENERATED AS IDENTITY can be called what PostgreSQL calls it without colliding with an unrelated concept in the same workspace."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-21T11:48:41.961788Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-21T12:28:06.756925Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Delivered: ColumnSpec::Identity(IdentityGeneration { Always, ByDefault }) with IdentityGeneration::keyword() giving the ALWAYS / BY DEFAULT words information_schema.columns.identity_generation reports; ColumnDef::identity() and identity_by_default() beside auto_increment(), whose doc now names identity as the recommended form. Renders as a column clause in CREATE TABLE and ADD COLUMN, and as ALTER COLUMN \"c\" ADD GENERATED .. AS IDENTITY on modify. Exclusivity verdict: typed where it is local (which form is a closed pair, not a bool), documented where it is not. Identity vs Default/Generated/AutoIncrement are four separate explicit calls rather than a flag the API offers — unlike the stored: false that Generated used to advertise, nothing presents the combination as a choice — and collapsing them into one slot would move DEFAULT out of the insertion-ordered spec list the rule itself fixes, breaking every interleaved render. So the combinations render, the grammar takes them, the server refuses each by name, and tests/identity_column_tests.rs holds those three refusals so the boundary is checked rather than asserted. Sequence options ((START WITH ..) and the rest) boundary-noted in the rule: a sequence definition wearing a column clause, left to raw_suffix until a typed sequence builder exists. Derive/codegen scope NOT taken and now recorded: pgorm-codegen has no identity introspection at all — it refuses an identity clause by name (\"unsupported DDL: an identity clause on column ..\", sql_schema_tests.rs:293) — so there is no round-trip to complete, and #[pgorm(identity)] would need an entity-model concept beside auto_increment plus a PrimaryKeyTrait change, which is the auto_increment migration this node deliberately does not start. Incidental: the commit's function-density gate on column.rs (58 cap, at its pre-image) forced room, so the interval field/precision vocabulary moved to table/interval.rs, re-exported, no path change. sql.ddl.column-def +4 -> +5, 11 sites repinned; the bump also retired the stale Extra/extra wording left by query-raw-template-words."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-21T12:28:09.876693Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

