            {
                id set-operation-grouping
                title "Chained set operations group in call order, so a.union(b).intersect(c) means (a UNION b) INTERSECT c, in Rust, Python and JavaScript"
                kind @fix
                deps (python-select-set-operations)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-18T19:21:23.735601Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by python-select-set-operations (e4152e18). The builders write set-operation arms in call order with no grouping, so PostgreSQL's precedence decides the meaning: INTERSECT binds tighter than UNION and EXCEPT, and a.union(b).intersect(c) runs as a UNION (b INTERSECT c). A live test confirms it returns {1} where reading the chain left to right gives the empty set. This holds in Rust (SelectStatement::union), Python and JavaScript, and neither sql.ast.select.union nor the bindings' docs mention it. A builder chain reads left to right, so make the rendered SQL mean that: when an arm is appended with an operator that binds tighter than an earlier one, parenthesize everything before it (render ((a UNION b)) INTERSECT c or the equivalent PostgreSQL accepts, checking that parenthesized set-operation operands with their own ORDER BY / LIMIT still render correctly). Hold it with oracle-checked renders and live tests in all three languages for each operator pair, and update sql.ast.select.union, the render rule and the bindings' docs. The lead's recommendation, filed for the operator to confirm: the alternative is to keep PostgreSQL's precedence and document it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-18T20:20:15.205633Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator 2026-10-18, offered either making chained set operations group in call order (the lead's recommendation: the builder parenthesizes what came before when a tighter-binding operator follows, changing the rendered SQL of affected queries) or keeping PostgreSQL's precedence and documenting it: \"as you recommend\". So the builders group left to right in Rust, Python and JavaScript."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-18T22:36:40.246465Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

