            {
                id update-nothing-distinct
                title "An update with nothing to set is distinguishable from matching nothing"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-19T23:13:22.776899Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "UpdateMany::exec returns Ok(0) without sending any statement when the value list is empty (src/executor/update.rs:61-67), and exec_returning_models returns Ok(vec![]) the same way (:85-87); neither doc mentions it. Ok(0) therefore conflates \"the WHERE matched nothing\" with \"you gave me nothing to SET\" — and the input is easy to produce, since UpdateMany::set skips Unchanged/NotSet fields (src/query/update.rs:216), so an ActiveModel round-tripped from the database yields exactly this. The asymmetry proves the design intent: singular exec_returning_model handles the same case by issuing a SELECT and returning the row (:33-35), and DeleteMany has no short-circuit at all (src/executor/delete.rs:29). Fix: make the empty-set case an Err, or align with the singular path's semantics."
