    {
        id tokenizer-backslash-standard
        title "The tokenizer honours backslash escapes only where PostgreSQL does"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-19T23:55:14.421271Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found during query-tokenizer-postgres (38b05af9) and deliberately left out of that commit: the tokenizer treats a backslash as an escape inside every quoted form, but PostgreSQL gives backslash meaning only in E'...' strings. Under standard_conforming_strings a plain 'C:\\' is a complete literal, so inject_parameters scanning WHERE a = 'C:\\' AND b = $1 believes the literal is still open and swallows the $1 marker — the same defect shape as the deleted backtick handling. Recorded explicitly in the sql.token.limits+2 limitations list, so the spec currently admits the gap. Fixing it means sql.token.quoted and sql.token.unquote change together: backslash is ordinary inside '...' and \"...\", an escape only inside E'...'. The tokenizer's one consumer pairing matters — inject_parameters both scans and re-renders, so the unquote side must match or a round-trip corrupts the literal."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-23T22:47:40.761687Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-23T23:26:31.551166Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id prqlc-literal-ordinal
        title "A constant key in GROUP BY or ORDER BY is never read as a column position"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-23T22:42:20.48433Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found 2026-09-24 by the agent fixing prqlc-group-by-wildcard in the fork. PostgreSQL reads a bare integer literal in GROUP BY as an output-column position, not a constant, so `from cake | select {id, name} | derive {x = 5} | group this (aggregate …)` compiles to `GROUP BY id, name, 5` and fails at execution with 42P10 \"GROUP BY position 5 is not in select list\". Verified against the live PostgreSQL 16 server. PostgreSQL gives ORDER BY the same positional reading, so a sort key that lowers to an integer literal is the likely sibling; DISTINCT ON's key list is to be checked too. Reachable from pgorm through the prql! macro, which compiles raw PRQL text through the fork. The fix belongs in the fork on branch pgorm/deterministic-star-expansion and lands in pgorm through the same re-pin that carries prqlc-group-by-wildcard's fix (dd9a0fee), so pgorm re-pins once."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T14:38:45.551826Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

