    {
        id identifier-injection
        title "Every identifier position is tested for injection by an instrument that can fire there"
        kind @test
        children (
            {
                id identifier-render-oracle
                title "Every identifier render site round-trips a hostile name as exactly one identifier"
                implements {
                    main ([spec:pgorm:req:security.ident-oracle] [spec:pgorm:req:security.ident-oracle.nul])
                }
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-24T18:44:29.47518Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "The sqlmap suite is structurally unable to fire in identifier positions: six full-profile cases (pipeline-projection, schema, column, group, function, stored-identifier) have no scheduled technique at all and five more (graph-alias, table, alias, order, enum-ddl) only U and/or S, because a level-3 boundary cannot close a faithful double-quoted identifier and attach a payload (security/sqlmap/CONTEXTS.md, acceptance 2026-09-14 \"what this does not claim\"). Yet identifiers are where ORM injection lives — two real holes were found and fixed by hand this month (pipeline identifiers under registry prqlc, f48c6425; pgorm-pool savepoint names, 276dde57). The instrument that can fire everywhere is structural rather than behavioural: for every public API that renders a caller-supplied name into SQL text, render a corpus of hostile names — embedded `\"`, `\\\"`, `\"\"`, NUL, `;`, `--`, `/*`, `$1`, `$$`, unicode including combining marks and right-to-left, 63/64-byte boundaries, keywords, empty — and parse the output with libpg_query (pg_query, already a dependency of pgorm's sql! macro and paginator). Assert the name comes back as exactly one identifier node carrying exactly the input text at the expected position, the statement count is one, and the parse tree's shape equals that of the same statement rendered with a benign name. A name the API refuses (the pipeline's UnquotableIdentifier, NUL) must be refused, never rendered. The site list is an explicit registry the oracle iterates, and a new identifier-bearing API is incomplete until it is registered — scope-collate, scope-sequences, scope-composite-types, scope-on-constraint and scope-merge depend on this node for exactly that reason. Add a spec rule stating the property and the registry obligation, and update security/sqlmap's \"does not claim\" text to point at this instrument for the positions sqlmap cannot reach."
                    }

