    {
        id type-part-keyword-names
        title "A keyword passed as a name renders bare where TypeName's part policy applies"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-24T19:51:27.708282Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found by the identifier render oracle (tests/identifier_oracle_tests.rs, node identifier-render-oracle). `TypeName::prepare_part` (pgorm-query/src/types.rs) writes a part bare whenever it matches `^[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*$`, and that pattern admits SQL keywords, which are not identifiers. Every site under that policy — `Func::named` (in a projection and as a FROM function), `Expr::cast_as` / `cast_as_type` (bare, schema-qualified, array), `Expr::as_enum`, `ColumnType::named`, `ColumnDef::named` with a schema, `ColumnDef::array`, `ColumnDef::enumeration`, `IndexType::Named` — therefore hands a keyword name to the grammar as a keyword. One case changes the statement's shape: `Query::select().expr(Func::named(Name::runtime(\"not\")).arg(1))` renders `SELECT not(1)`, which libpg_query parses as a BoolExpr NOT over 1 rather than a FuncCall of a function named `not` (the same class reaches `row(..)` → RowExpr, `coalesce`/`nullif`/`greatest`/`least`, `exists`/`array` over a subquery, and `only(\"t\")` in FROM-function position → `FROM ONLY t`, per the pgorm-query audit). The rest are refusals by the grammar: `select`, `from`, `user`, `not` at every type position (`SELECT CAST(1 AS select)`), `integer` as a function name (`SELECT integer(1)`) or schema qualifier (`CAST(1 AS integer.ty)`), `left` as an index access method (`USING left`) — so a function, type or access method genuinely named with a keyword cannot be referenced through these APIs at all. This contradicts [spec:pgorm:def:sql.types.type-name] (\"A name is therefore a name: text that is not an identifier becomes a quoted identifier PostgreSQL refuses, never SQL it executes\") and [spec:pgorm:req:sql.render.ident-quoting]. The type-sugar reading the rule intends (`integer` as a type resolving to pg_catalog.int4) is not part of the defect; the oracle accepts it as its own verdict. Direction, not prescription: quote any part PostgreSQL's keyword list does not classify as unreserved, except where the position's grammar needs the bare keyword (type sugar); a fixed list pinned against libpg_query's keyword table would keep the policy total. Pinned in tests/identifier_oracle/pins.rs (49 site × name pairs); retire the pins with the fix."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T22:20:41.546117Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T22:48:03.368721Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id pipeline-bare-dollar-names
        title "Pipeline names beginning with $, and a lone *, reach SQL bare through prqlc"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-24T20:13:35.735754Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found by the identifier render oracle (tests/identifier_oracle_tests.rs, node identifier-render-oracle) at every pipeline identifier site: `Pipeline::from`, `from_schema` (both parts), `col` (both parts), `this`, `that`, a bare `Name` in filter/sort/group, `join`/`append` relations, `as_runtime`, `named_runtime`, the `select_sources` qualifier, and an entity's runtime table name. pgorm's screen at `Pipeline::into_sql` refuses only `\"` and NUL (`unquotable`, src/pipeline/error.rs) and hands every other name to prqlc, which writes it bare whenever its `valid_ident` (`^((\\*)|([a-z_$][a-z0-9_$]*))$`, prqlc/src/sql/utils) accepts it and it is not a keyword. A leading `$` and a lone `*` pass, and PostgreSQL's lexer reads them as something other than a name. Rendered SQL: `Pipeline::from(t).select(col(t, Name::runtime(\"$1\")))` → `SELECT $1 FROM t`, a ParamRef (bound parameter 1) where the benign tree has a ColumnRef, so the projection reads a value instead of a column; `Pipeline::from(t).group(Name::runtime(\"$$\")).aggregate(count_rows())` → `SELECT $$, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY $$`, whose two names open and close one dollar-quoted string that swallows `, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY ` — the FROM and GROUP BY vanish, and a WHERE between two such names (a tenant filter) would vanish the same way; `$tag$` behaves identically; `col(t, Name::runtime(\"*\"))` → `SELECT * FROM t`, the wildcard where a column named `*` was asked for. Elsewhere the same names are syntax errors (`SELECT * FROM $1`, `FROM *.t`). It also falsifies src/pipeline/census.rs's premise that every `$N` in the output was minted by the binder: census would count and renumber an identifier-born `$N`. Python's `validate_name` admits the same names, so pgorm.pipeline reaches it too. The pgorm-query path is unaffected: it quotes unconditionally, and the oracle round-trips these names at all 104 of its identifier sites. Direction, not prescription: stop relying on prqlc's conditional quoting for caller names — refuse a leading `$` and a lone `*` in the screen (a change to [spec:pgorm:req:pipeline.errors]), or have the fork quote any name its lexer-safety test does not match PostgreSQL's (`$` is only safe after the first character). Pinned in tests/identifier_oracle/pins.rs (72 site × name pairs); retire the pins with the fix."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T20:14:59.488099Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
