    {
        id pipeline-read-cast-verbatim
        title "select_sources writes a column's read-cast type name into SQL verbatim"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-24T20:13:35.800801Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found by the identifier render oracle (site `pgorm/pipeline.select-sources.read-cast`, tests/identifier_oracle/registry_orm.rs). `Pipeline::select_sources` projects each column through `source_read_cast` (src/query/graph.rs), which reads the cast target of the column's `ColumnTrait::select_as` back as `TypeName::raw_text()` — the unquoted, dotted spelling — and `project_into` (src/pipeline/sources.rs) passes that text to prqlc as `adapter::ident(&cast)` in the type slot of `std.as`. That slot is `noresolve.type`, and prqlc lowers an unresolved ident by writing its name verbatim, so the type name becomes SQL. With a hand-written `select_as` returning `expr.cast_as(Name::runtime(ty))`: `ty = \";\"` → `SELECT CAST(id AS ;) AS s0_id FROM t`; `\"--\"` → `CAST(id AS --) AS s0_id FROM t` (the rest commented out); `\"a b\"` → `CAST(id AS a b)`; `\"MixedCase\"` → folded to `mixedcase`; `\"\"` → `CAST(id AS )`; and a type text such as `int4) AS s0_id, (SELECT current_user) AS leak, CAST(1 AS int4` would add a projection. Only `\"` and NUL are refused, by the pipeline's identifier screen. The same column rendered through `SelectGraph`/`Select` goes through pgorm-query's `TypeName` part policy and is quoted, so the two writers disagree about one column. Reach: only a hand-written `select_as` whose type text is computed at run time — the enum default (`text`/`text[]`) and `#[pgorm(select_as = \"…\")]` are program text — but `select_as` is a public trait method, and the documented claim that a name in cast position is quoted ([spec:pgorm:def:sql.types.type-name]) does not hold on this path. Direction: pass the `TypeName`'s parts to prqlc as quoted identifier parts (or render the cast through pgorm-query's policy) rather than as raw text in a noresolve slot. Pinned in tests/identifier_oracle/pins.rs; retire the pin with the fix."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T21:46:39.555288Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T22:19:07.367803Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id identifier-bypass-surfaces
        title "No public field or deref route renders a runtime name past its quoting"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-24T21:09:50.767839Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found by the identifier-render-oracle audit (967b4120) and recorded there as found-but-not-registered; these two are real routes around a quoting guarantee rather than raw SQL by design. (1) `TypeName`'s `verbatim` field is public, so a caller can build a quoted `TypeName` from runtime text and flip it to verbatim by field assignment, defeating the `&'static str` bound that `TypeName::raw` exists to enforce (the raw/template vocabulary, 10a04f40). Make the field private, keep `raw()` as the only verbatim constructor, and check every in-tree reader goes through an accessor. (2) `pgorm_pool::Transaction` derefs to `tokio_postgres::Transaction`, whose own `savepoint(name)` interpolates the name unquoted into `SAVEPOINT {name}` through the simple protocol — so `PgTransaction::savepoint(&mut *txn, name)` bypasses the quoting 276dde57 added to pgorm's wrapper. Either remove the `Deref`/`DerefMut` (and give pgorm-pool the methods callers actually need), or keep it and make the bypass impossible to reach by accident; decide by what in-tree code uses through the deref. Register whatever remains reachable in the oracle. Out of scope, by design or inherent: `String::leak` satisfying `&'static str`, `SqlTemplate` accepting runtime text (it binds values and validates markers), `RecyclingMethod::Custom` (caller configuration), and `SqlName::prepare` being overridable by an implementor (their own type, their own quoting)."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T22:48:44.96865Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-24T23:28:56.9273Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id searched-case-needs-arm
        title "A searched CASE cannot be built without its first arm"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-25T02:36:03.020168Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found while building scope-simple-case (a885cd14). `CaseStatement::new()` constructs a searched CASE with no WHEN arm, so `Query::select().expr(CaseStatement::new().finally(\"x\"))` renders `SELECT (CASE ELSE 'x' END)`, which the grammar rejects; pinned today in pgorm-query/tests/postgres/oracle_pins.rs (~line 613) and named as a limitation in sql.ast.case. The new simple form already rules this out by construction — `Expr::case_of(operand)` returns a `CaseOperand` with no conversion into an expression until its first `when` — and the searched form should match: the only public way to begin a searched CASE takes its first condition and result. Remove or privatise the armless `new()`, sweep its callers (grep all crates, codegen output and pgorm-python included), unpin the oracle case, and update sql.ast.case (bump, repin)."
            }

            {
