            {
                id python-ranges
                title "Python reads, writes and builds with the built-in ranges and multiranges: a Range class, tagged values, decoded columns, stubs"
                kind @feat
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T11:10:51.888539Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T11:19:17.720063Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T11:22:37.449817Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Range classes are native (pgorm.Range, pgorm.Multirange registered in _native, typed in _ranges.pyi), not pure Python: the value conversion reads and builds them without importing the package that loads the extension, which also lets the binding's Rust tests round-trip them under an embedded interpreter. Kinds are the PostgreSQL type names (int4range … tstzmultirange), so a range slots into the existing Value / Value.null / Value.array / snapshot tag mechanism with no new constructor. Python's Expr does NOT gain contains/contained/overlaps: _expressions.pyi's Expr stub sits at the 50-function density cap (adding them was refused by the gate), and a class's stub cannot be split across sibling files, so a range predicate is RawSQL with bound range values (tested). A future way to reach them would be a generic binary-operator method replacing several of Expr's one-operator methods, or a module-level function family in its own stub."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-10-05T00:17:24.301434Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Filed when scope-range-types landed (6006221d), which built range values for PostgreSQL's six built-in range types and refused, by name, every consumer listed here. These items could use ranges; until now they only handle them. (1) Arrays of ranges: `Vec<Range<T>>` / `Vec<Multirange<T>>` have no NotU8, ValueType or TryGetable impl, so an entity field of `int4range[]` does not compile. Codegen refuses `int4range[]` for that reason. `Value::array([..])` already binds and renders one. Live-checked: `($1::int4range[])[3]` reads back through the bind path and the inline literal path alike. (2) Range types a schema creates over a subtype that is not one of the six (the PG docs' floatrange is `Range<f64>`). RangeType and RangeElement are closed to the built-ins, and a created type's name is unknown to the Rust type, so supporting this needs a tag that names the created type. (3) Multiranges a schema creates. tokio-postgres's TYPEINFO query joins pg_range on rngtypid, so it reports such a type (typtype 'm') as Kind::Simple, and pgorm refuses it in both directions (live-tested: slot_multirange). This needs a driver-side type lookup or an explicit subtype supplied by the caller. (4) Codegen refuses `CREATE TYPE ... AS RANGE` by name, and a column of a created range type is unsupported. (5) Python refuses ranges by name: the capability manifest lists range and multirange as unsupported, decode raises DecodeError, and PyValue::from_rust raises UnsupportedCapabilityError. Support needs a Python range class, new snapshot tags, type stubs and the wheel acceptance suites. (6) Expr has contains() for @> and contained() for <@, but no builder for && (only BinOper::Overlap through Expr::binary). An element-containment predicate needs its operand pinned with cast_as, because PostgreSQL types `r @> $1`'s $1 as the range. Spec homes: sql.value.range, exec.decode.range, exec.cursor.binding-range, sql.ddl.type-range, python.values."
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-07T10:38:10.158014Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Narrowed 2026-10-07 and split into two children, each a feat commit of its own: range-arrays-and-overlap (pieces 1 and 6 of the filing note: Vec<Range<T>> / Vec<Multirange<T>> entity fields, codegen for int4range[], and Expr::overlaps for &&) and python-ranges (piece 5). Pieces 2-4 hinge on one design question — how pgorm names a range type a schema created, in Value's range tag and in ColumnType — and are filed as created-range-types (values over other subtypes, codegen for CREATE TYPE ... AS RANGE and its columns) and created-multiranges (blocked by the driver's type lookup; routes recorded there). They are separate root nodes, not children, so this node completes with what it delivers."
            }
        )
    }

