            {
                id pg17-sql-json
                title "SQL/JSON query functions and JSON_TABLE are typed: JSON_QUERY, JSON_VALUE, JSON_EXISTS, JSON_TABLE, and the JSON constructors"
                kind @feat
                deps (pg18-test-server)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-06T12:50:41.556394Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PostgreSQL 17's SQL/JSON: JSON_QUERY, JSON_VALUE and JSON_EXISTS (with PASSING, RETURNING, wrapper and quotes behaviour, ON EMPTY / ON ERROR), JSON_TABLE as a FROM item (COLUMNS, PATH, NESTED, ORDINALITY, EXISTS columns), and the constructors JSON(), JSON_SCALAR(), JSON_SERIALIZE(). pgorm-query's JSON support today is operators only (pgorm-query/src/expr_json.rs, sql.ast.expr.json). The constructors parse as ordinary calls and are reachable through Func::named; the query functions and JSON_TABLE are not, since Func::named cannot spell their clauses. These are 17 grammar, which pg_query 6.2.0 already parses (verified), so this does not wait on pg-query-18. JSON path strings are values and must be bound, never interpolated (check whether PostgreSQL accepts a bound path; if a path must be a literal, render it through the value pipeline's literal escaping and say so in the rule). Operator scope reading: \"support it all\", so build it. If it turns out large, split JSON_TABLE into its own node."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-06T17:10:35.842467Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator 2026-10-06, after asking what SQL/JSON is: \"all of it, and replace any older stuff with it if that's sensible to do.\" Build the whole of PostgreSQL's SQL/JSON:\n- JSON_QUERY, JSON_VALUE and JSON_EXISTS, with PASSING, RETURNING, wrapper and quotes behaviour, and ON EMPTY / ON ERROR\n- JSON_TABLE as a FROM item: COLUMNS, PATH, NESTED PATH, FOR ORDINALITY, EXISTS columns, ON ERROR\n- the 17 constructors JSON(), JSON_SCALAR() and JSON_SERIALIZE()\n- the 16 forms if missing: JSON_OBJECT, JSON_ARRAY, JSON_OBJECTAGG, JSON_ARRAYAGG and IS JSON\n\nThe existing operators live in pgorm-query/src/expr_json.rs:\n- get_json_field / cast_json_field (-> / ->>, then a cast)\n- get_json_path / cast_json_path (#> / #>>)\n- has_json_key / has_any_json_keys / has_all_json_keys (? / ?| / ?&)\n\nReplace one only where the SQL/JSON form gives the same semantics and the same index use, verified live with EXPLAIN on PostgreSQL 18. Two checks matter:\n- whether a GIN jsonb_ops / jsonb_path_ops index that serves ?, ?|, ?& and @> also serves JSON_EXISTS\n- whether an expression index on (data->>'k') or ((data->>'k')::int) is still used for JSON_VALUE(... RETURNING ...)\n\nCompare semantics too: ? also matches string array elements; a failed cast throws while JSON_VALUE follows ON ERROR; and behaviour differs on a missing path and on a path that hits an object or array.\n\nThe lead's expectation, unverified, is that indexes make wholesale replacement wrong, so both families stay and the rule says which to use when. The evidence decides, and the rule records it. Any old API removed is deleted outright, with no shims, and every caller (pipeline, pgorm-python, the campaign emitters) is updated."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T08:05:31.635466Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "2026-10-07: JSON_TABLE split into its own node, pg17-json-table, as the operator's note allowed: it is a FROM item rather than an expression, with its own column grammar and literal-only paths, and the rest of SQL/JSON is one coherent commit without it. This node keeps the query functions, the constructors, IS JSON and the operator-replacement verdict; sql.scope records JSON_TABLE as deferred until pg17-json-table lands. Python's binding is filed as python-sql-json."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T08:05:57.948667Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T08:39:18.440693Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id pg19-test-server
                title "After PostgreSQL 19's general release, the test server moves to 19 and what it adds is surveyed"
                kind @test
                deps (pg18-security-baselines)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-06T12:50:41.611182Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "PostgreSQL 19's GA is planned for 2026-10-29 (RC1 2026-10-15). Do not start before GA. Then move the test server and every pin to 19, survey 19's final release notes against pgorm's typed surface as was done for 17/18 (beta 4 reverted SQL/PGQ, FOR PORTION OF, partition MERGE/SPLIT, the pg_get_*_ddl functions and online checksum changes, so check what actually shipped), check whether libpg_query and pg_query.rs have a 19 grammar, and file what it finds as nodes."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T15:27:46.549863Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator 2026-10-07: \"what's stopping us from doing some preliminary tests on the rc branch? pg_query? If nothing, we can have a pg-19 branch to test this one.\" Nothing stops it: libpg_query has no 19 grammar yet (no branch or tag past 18.1.0), but that only matters for 19-only syntax, which pgorm does not emit, so the 18 grammar covers everything pgorm writes. Decision: preliminary testing runs now against the newest 19 build (19beta4, postgres:19beta4-bookworm, container pgorm-test-19 on port 54319, separate from the 18.6 pgorm-test server) on branch pg-19, then again on RC1 (2026-10-15). pg-19 merges to main only after 19's GA (2026-10-29) and a re-run on RC1; this node completes on main after GA, not on the branch."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T15:27:50.534451Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T15:48:36.958428Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Preliminary run on PostgreSQL 19beta4, 2026-10-07, branch pg-19 from 5893893d. Server: container pgorm-test-19, postgres:19beta4-bookworm (sha256:4c618d9a30e0), \"PostgreSQL 19beta4 (Debian 19~beta4-1.pgdg12+1) on aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu\", port 54319. Its data sits under /var/lib/postgresql/19/docker, as 18 keeps it, so the volume mounts at /var/lib/postgresql. Before adapting anything: nextest 1764/1766 (main is 1766/1766 on 18.6); pgorm-query doctests with every feature 431 + 64 (2 ignored) and pgorm's 167 (2 ignored) all pass; pgorm-python 122/124 (124/124 on a disposable 18.6 through tests/with_postgres.py at the same commit); the application check (checks/entities.py) passes; nplan check 31/31. Every failure is one of three 19 behaviour changes, each confirmed against a disposable 18.6 container. (1) ALTER CONSTRAINT .. ENFORCED / NOT ENFORCED applies to a CHECK, which 18 refuses (42809). ENFORCED checks the rows (23514) and leaves the constraint valid; NOT ENFORCED leaves it not valid. Keys, NOT NULL and EXCLUDE stay 42809. (2) SET EXPRESSION on a virtual column is no longer refused under a CHECK constraint (18: 0A000). There is no rewrite; the rows are checked against the table's CHECKs (23514). A publication still refuses it (0A000). (3) JSON_ARRAY(query) over no rows is [] (18: NULL); no test asserted it. These are tests and spec text that pinned 18's behaviour, not pgorm bugs. They were adapted on pg-19: the tests, sql.ddl.alter-table, sql.ddl.enforcement, sql.ast.expr.sql-json, the rustdoc and pgorm-python/SCHEMA.md. No rule version was bumped, as no builder changed. After: nextest 1768/1768 and pgorm-python 124/124 on 19beta4. Exactly the three new assertions fail on 18.6 (checked), so pg-19 merges only with the move of the server. Also checked on 19beta4: standard_conforming_strings is forced on. SET .. off is 0A000, while ALTER ROLE .. SET .. on, as the sqlmap and campaign fixtures do, is accepted, and escape_string_warning is gone. Both JSON server-bug canaries (JSON_SERIALIZE over jsonb, and #19695) still see their bugs. 19 adds five unreserved keywords (ignore, lsn, repack, respect, wait); ignore and respect are not bare column labels, but pgorm writes AS before every label, and the restricted set is unchanged. The changed defaults (jit off, max_locks_per_transaction 128, default_toast_compression lz4, log_lock_waits on) and the refusal of CR/LF in database and role names touch nothing pgorm asserts. The ambiguous-operator error moved \"Could not choose a best candidate operator\" from HINT to DETAIL; nothing asserts it. Not run: sqlmap and the generative campaign, which wait for RC1 or GA on a quiet machine. Typed surface: ON CONFLICT DO SELECT and window IGNORE/RESPECT NULLS are filed under postgres-19, both behind pg-query-19 (libpg_query has no 19 grammar yet). The rest reaches pgorm through Func::named and casts: oid8, uuid/bytea casts, random(min, max) over dates and timestamps, base64url and base32hex, error_on_null, range_minus_multi and multirange_minus_multi, tid_block and tid_offset, the jsonpath string methods, and IS JSON over domains. Some additions are outside pgorm's scope: REPACK, WAIT, GRANT .. GRANTED BY, CHECKPOINT options, publication EXCEPT and ALL SEQUENCES, and COPY TO JSON / ON_ERROR SET_NULL. Re-run on RC1 (2026-10-15) and again at GA."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T18:08:10.8234Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator 2026-10-07, after 8e3fdece changed three test expectations to PostgreSQL 19's answers unconditionally: \"so those changes should be gated behind a pg-19 feature. This is how we can maintain backwards compat in a typesafe way. pg-18 will be our default (i.e. doesn't need to be stated).\" Decision: a cargo feature pg-19. Without it pgorm targets PostgreSQL 18, the floor (there is no pg-18 feature, and no 16/17 support to preserve). With it pgorm targets 19: the builder offers 19-only surface and the documented and tested behaviour is 19's. Features stay additive: pg-19 may add API and change the documented server behaviour, never remove 18 API that 19 still accepts. The three adaptations of 8e3fdece are redone under the gate (18's answer by default, 19's under cfg(feature = \"pg-19\")), and each difference is gated in the type wherever the builder knows enough to tell, documented per version where it cannot. The sql!/prql! grammar and the render oracle stay on the 18 grammar until pg-query-19 lands."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T18:41:21.195717Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "pg-19 feature landed on branch pg-19 (2026-10-07), per the operator's decision above; the model is rule sql.target (docs/spec/sql-ast.md). Crates: pgorm-query declares pg-19 = [], pgorm forwards it (pg-19 = [\"pgorm-query/pg-19\"]), pgorm-python forwards it (pg-19 = [\"pgorm/pg-19\"]) and its manifest's features list pg-19 when pgorm targets 19; pgorm_query::POSTGRES_TARGET (18 | 19) is the target a dependent can read. pgorm-macros, pgorm-codegen, pgorm-sql-macro and pgorm-migration do not carry it: nothing of theirs differs yet (sql-macro and codegen take it with pg-query-19). Type gating: none of the three differences can be, each turning on server knowledge no builder value records (the kind of constraint a name holds; the table's CHECKs; the row count of JSON_ARRAY's query), so each is documented per release in the rule and the rustdoc, and its live test holds 18's answer by default and 19's under cfg(feature = \"pg-19\"); a CHECK-only alter method was weighed and not built, since it would assert a kind the type cannot check and render the SQL alter_constraint already renders. No decode type or doc promised either JSON_ARRAY answer. Checks: platform rust-pg-19 (workspace check and clippy, every target, with pg-19; pgorm's lib with no defaults and macros only, each with pg-19) and python-native's check-pg-19. CI: nothing on 19 until GA. There is no Rust live-test job at all to extend (nextest is a local gate), PGDG apt carries postgresql-19 19~beta4 only in noble-pgdg's 19 component (a floating beta with no pin), and Homebrew has no 19; at GA this node moves the server and CI's PostgreSQL pins, and the pg-19 suite runs locally against pgorm-test-19 until then. Results: nextest without the feature on 18.6 1769/1769 (main's 1766 + set_expression_under_a_check + the two target_release_tests), with pg-19 on 19beta4 1770/1770 (+ alter_constraint_enforces_a_check); pgorm-python default on a disposable 18.6 124/124 and pg-19 on 19beta4 124/124. Each way round, against the wrong server exactly the version-dependent tests fail: 3 Rust tests either way, and test_schema's two Python tests either way. pgorm-query doctests 431 + 65 by default (POSTGRES_TARGET's example is compile_fail E0080 there) and 432 + 64 with every feature (the same example compiles and runs)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-18T13:46:34.407724Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Preliminary run on PostgreSQL 19 RC1, 2026-10-18, main at ad348f7d. The release dates moved: RC1 was announced 2026-10-15, RC2 is planned for 2026-11-12 and general availability for 2026-11-19, not 2026-10-29 as noted above. Server: pgorm-test-19 recreated on a fresh pgorm-test-19-data volume from postgres:19rc1-bookworm@sha256:228dc3e5a9bd18343efeecc7ae3f52aee9b6cf3a9dca5159ece1eef254a80f06, \"PostgreSQL 19rc1 (Debian 19~rc1-1.pgdg12+1) on aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu\", port 54319; CLAUDE.md now names that image. Results against beta 4's adapted run: cargo nextest run --workspace --features pg-19 is 1822/1822 (beta 4: 1768/1768; the difference is the tests main gained since 2026-10-07, and nothing fails), including the pg-19 tests that hold 19's three behaviour changes (ALTER CONSTRAINT .. ENFORCED on a CHECK, SET EXPRESSION under a CHECK, JSON_ARRAY(query) over no rows giving []). Both JSON server-bug canaries (JSON_SERIALIZE over jsonb, and #19695) still see their bugs. pgorm-python built with pg-19 (its capabilities list pg-19) is 178/178 through tests/with_postgres.py's TLS fixture with its image switched to the RC1 digest (beta 4: 124/124). Against the bare pgorm-test-19 the six TLS tests added on 2026-10-09 error, because that container has TLS off; that is the setup, not 19. The application check (checks/entities.py) passes. The server checks give beta 4's answers: SET standard_conforming_strings = off is 0A000 while ALTER ROLE .. SET .. on is accepted, escape_string_warning is gone, and the keyword list differs from 18.6 by the same five unreserved words (ignore and respect are not bare labels). RC1's announced fixes since beta 4 (REPACK CONCURRENTLY, pg_plan_advice, planner optimisations that depend on whether an expression can be NULL, logical replication switching itself on, postgres_fdw statistics import, parallel autovacuum, COPY .. FORMAT json keys) change nothing pgorm's tests see. Not run: the doctests, sqlmap and the generative campaign, which wait for general availability."
