            {
                id merge-beyond-pg16
                title "MERGE reaches what scope-merge refused by name: PostgreSQL 17's BY SOURCE arms, RETURNING and CTE body"
                kind @feat
                deps (pg18-test-server)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-05T02:02:07.525086Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed when scope-merge landed (60175b87). That node built MERGE to the grammar PostgreSQL 15 and 16 share and refused the rest by name. (1) PostgreSQL 17's MERGE grammar, now the sql.scope Deferred entry \"MERGE's PostgreSQL 17 grammar\". All three parts were checked on a throwaway postgres:17 container (17.9). (a) WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE is a third arm kind, for target rows that no source row matched. It takes UPDATE, DELETE or DO NOTHING; INSERT there is 42601. It needs a third MergeArms slot with its own action type (UPDATE/DELETE/DO NOTHING over target columns only), and BY TARGET as the explicit spelling of today's not-matched arm. (b) RETURNING, with merge_action() naming the action each row took ('INSERT'/'UPDATE'/'DELETE'). It needs a returning slot on MergeStatement and a merge_action() function. merge_action() is valid only inside a MERGE's RETURNING, so a typed home for it is the open question. (c) MERGE as a CTE body. 17 accepts it even without RETURNING, but MERGE is still 42601 as an expression subquery. It needs a SubQueryStatement::MergeStatement arm and an IntoSubQueryStatement impl. CommonTableExpression::new is the only caller that needs it, and Expr::SubQuery would then admit a MERGE that the server refuses there. On 16 each part is 42601 or 0A000, and the builder cannot know which release it writes for (sql.ddl.column-def's VIRTUAL precedent), so the trigger is the test server moving to 17 or a decision to gate on the server version. (2) Python: pgorm-python exposes no MERGE (python.statements asks for SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE with conflict handling), and a raw-SQL path already reaches it. A binding would need native objects for PendingMerge/MergeStatement, MergeUpdate, MergeInsert and the two action enums, capability-manifest entries, type stubs, and parity cases in checks/direct_builders.py. (3) The ORM is deliberately not listed. ON CONFLICT covers the upsert an entity layer issues, and MERGE on 16 has no RETURNING, which every model-returning write terminal needs. Once (1b) lands, a bulk \"sync this set\" terminal that reports per-row actions is the one ORM use worth weighing. Spec homes: sql.scope, sql.ast.merge, sql.render.merge, sql.ast, python.statements."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-06T12:50:55.435479Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "2026-10-06: moved under postgres-18 with a dep on pg18-test-server. This node's PostgreSQL 17 grammar (WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE, RETURNING with merge_action(), MERGE as a CTE body) becomes directly live-testable once the test server is 18, and pg_query 6.2.0 already parses it (17 grammar, verified by the survey), so it does not wait on pg-query-18. The Python-binding half is unchanged."
                    }

