            {
                id entity-temporal-keys
                title "An entity can declare a key WITHOUT OVERLAPS and a relation matching on a PERIOD, so codegen can read them"
                kind @feat
                deps (pg18-temporal-keys)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T02:56:36.865486Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by pg18-temporal-keys (d33e0801). PostgreSQL 18's temporal keys: a primary or unique key ending `c WITHOUT OVERLAPS` (a range or multirange compared for overlap, enforced by a GiST index as an exclusion: an overlap is 23P01, an empty period 23514, btree_gist needed for scalar columns) and a foreign key `FOREIGN KEY (a, PERIOD p) REFERENCES t (a, PERIOD q)` (the child's period covered by the union of the matching parents' periods; ON DELETE / ON UPDATE NO ACTION only, every other action 0A000). pgorm-query writes both (TableKey::without_overlaps, TableForeignKey / ForeignKeyCreateStatement::period; sql.ddl.create-table+14, sql.ddl.foreign-key+8) and Python's key builders take without_overlaps=. Codegen keeps refusing both by name (codegen.ddl.unsupported+10, \"a WITHOUT OVERLAPS key\", \"a PERIOD foreign key\"), and EntityTransformer::transform now refuses them in a caller-built statement (codegen.entity.transform+10), because the entity model matches by equality alone: DeriveEntityModel's primary_key / unique attributes, PrimaryKeyTrait and find_by_id, the ActiveModel's key for update and delete, RelationDef's from/to columns joined by equality in load_one / load_many and the graph, and Schema::create_table_from_entity's key and foreign-key output. Decide what a temporal key means to the ORM (is find_by_id over (id, period) an equality lookup, or a point-in-time lookup by containment? what do update and delete by primary key do when the period is part of the identity?), whether a relation can carry a PERIOD pair and how the loaders and the graph join over it (containment or overlap, `@>` / `&&`, rather than equality), and then the derive attributes, schema generation (which must also say how btree_gist gets created, or document it), codegen's reader and writer, the generative campaign's emitters, and Python's registered entities. entity-relation-not-enforced is the same kind of gap and may share the attribute design."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-08T20:37:11.278313Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-08T20:52:29.863804Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Decided 2026-10-08. A temporal primary key's value is the whole key, period included, and a lookup by it is by equality. Equal non-empty periods overlap, and an empty period is refused (23514), so at most one row holds a given key value. find_by_id, an update by key and a delete by key each name one version. A point-in-time read ('which version covers t') is a filter on the period (contains), not a key lookup, so no new API is built for it. The entity declares the key with #[pgorm(primary_key, without_overlaps)] on its last key field, and PrimaryKeyTrait::without_overlaps() (default false) reports it. The derive refuses the attribute on a non-key field, before another key field, or on a key with no other column (42601 on the server). A relation carries a PERIOD pair as a slot of ColumnPairs (RelationBuilder::period, derive from_period / to_period). The pair joins by overlap (&&), so a row reaches every version its period overlaps, which may be several. find_related and graph slots return each version. load_one keeps its HasOne contract: Some when one version overlaps, Err when several. load_many from the versions gives each version the rows it overlaps. from_key/to_key end with the period, so the loaders file rows of one key under their own periods. The derive refuses a period on one side, a period without from/to, and any action but NoAction (0A000 on 18). Schema generation writes WITHOUT OVERLAPS and PERIOD. It does not create btree_gist, which a scalar column beside the period needs (42704), so that is documented instead. Codegen reads a temporal primary key and a PERIOD foreign key. It still refuses a WITHOUT OVERLAPS unique key, since an entity's unique columns are single columns. It refuses a temporal key whose period column the table declares before another key column, since the compact derive needs the period last. The generative campaign's emitters and Python's registered entities are not extended here; they compile unchanged, and their coverage is filed as a follow-up."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-08T20:59:31.234043Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id orm-returning-old-new
                title "An entity update can return each row's previous model, and an upsert can say whether it inserted or updated"
                kind @feat
                deps (pg18-returning-old-new)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T04:56:03.512269Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Found by pg18-returning-old-new (947a062a). PostgreSQL 18's RETURNING reads a written row's old and new versions, and pgorm-query now writes both (ReturningRow, ReturningClause::old_as / new_as; sql.ast.returning+1). The ORM's write terminals still return only the new row: UpdateOne::exec and UpdateMany::exec_returning_models (src/executor/update.rs) and the insert terminals exec_returning_pk(s) / exec_returning_model(s) (src/executor/insert.rs) build their RETURNING list from ColumnTrait::into_returning_expr, unqualified, which reads the new row. Two uses are worth weighing, and neither is a one-liner:\n\n(1) An update terminal that returns each row's previous model beside its new one. The decode path reads one model per row, so a pair needs two prefixed model decodes from one row, as the graph's s{i}_ aliases do (bounded to 63 bytes by the shared composition), with old.\"col\" AS \"o_col\" and new.\"col\" AS \"n_col\" in the list.\n\n(2) An upsert (Insert with on_conflict DO UPDATE) that says per row whether it inserted or updated. Verified live on 18.6: a row ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE updated has the existing row as old, and a freshly inserted row's old is NULL in every column, so old.<primary key> IS NULL is exactly \"inserted\". That replaces the xmax = 0 folklore with a documented answer. A DO NOTHING row returns nothing, which the terminal has to report too.\n\nDecide the terminal shapes and their names in the exec vocabulary (exec.crud.exec-vocabulary), what they return for an entity whose table, or a FROM item of the statement, is called old or new (the bare keyword is then taken by that relation without complaint, sql.ast.returning, so the terminal would have to rename with WITH (OLD AS ..)), and whether Python's registered entities and the generative campaign's emitters gain them."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-08T21:51:49.718816Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-08T22:03:53.952646Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Decided 2026-10-09. Four terminals, named in the exec vocabulary (exec.crud.exec-vocabulary+2) as a fourth column, version-returning: UpdateOne::exec_returning_change -> Change<Model> { old, new }; UpdateMany::exec_returning_changes -> Vec<Change<Model>>; Insert<A, OneRow>::exec_returning_upsert -> Option<Upserted<Model>> (None for a row the statement did not write: DO NOTHING skipped it, or DO UPDATE's WHERE left it); Insert<A, ManyRows>::exec_returning_upserts -> Vec<Upserted<Model>> (skipped rows absent). Upserted is Inserted(M) | Updated(Change<M>), so an inserted row carries no fake old row; into_model() is the row as left. Inserted vs updated is read off the old version, PostgreSQL's documented answer: the old model decodes through from_query_result_optional, whose all-NULL witness is exactly 'no old row' since a primary key is NOT NULL. TryInsert gets no upsert terminal: its Empty/Conflicted would restate the upsert's own None/absence. The RETURNING list is every column of both versions through each column's select_as, aliased o_/n_ by result_column_name (the bounded 63-byte composition), which needed a named-item form in pgorm-query: Returning::expr_as / exprs_as, rendering <expr> AS \"<name>\" (sql.ast.returning+3, sql.render.returning+4, oracle site query/returning.expr-as). The versions are always renamed, RETURNING WITH (OLD AS \"pgorm_old\", NEW AS \"pgorm_new\"): live on 18.6 an entity whose table is old returned (2, 2) for old/new through the bare keywords, and an UPDATE .. FROM item called new captured new.col; with the rename the table called old returns its real old row, and a table called pgorm_old is refused 42712, loud instead of wrong. Live: a raced upsert (waiting on another transaction's uncommitted insert of its key) updates the committed row and reports Updated. Python's registered entities and the generative campaign are not extended here; filed as a follow-up."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-08T22:17:50.237394Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id python-merge-and-row-versions
                title "Python builds MERGE, and a RETURNING list can read and rename a written row's old and new versions"
                kind @feat
                deps (merge-beyond-pg16 pg18-returning-old-new)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T06:23:27.026241Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split from merge-beyond-pg16 (c5acfcaa), whose decision note says why the binding was not built there. pgorm-python exposes no MERGE (python.statements asks for SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE), and its INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE returning(*items) cannot read a written row's old and new versions or rename them, which pgorm-query now writes (ReturningRow, ReturningClause::old_as / new_as, sql.ast.returning+2; 947a062a). A raw statement reaches both today.\n\nMERGE needs native objects for the pending statement (no arm yet, so nothing to inspect or execute, as in Rust) and the statement; the three arm kinds, each with an optional condition (when_matched, when_not_matched, when_not_matched_by_source and their conditional forms, the unconditional arm of a kind rendering after its conditional arms, last call wins); the two action types (an update, a delete or nothing for a target row; an insert, a default-values insert or nothing for an unmatched source row, so an insert for a target row is refused by type or by an explicit TypeError); MergeUpdate / MergeInsert as non-empty (column, expression) pairs with overriding; with(), only(); and returning(*items) plus a returning_action flag, never a free merge_action() expression (it resolves only in a MERGE's RETURNING list, sql.ast.merge+1). The row versions need a Python spelling for (ReturningRow, column) and (ReturningRow, star), and the renames as keyword arguments of returning(). Each needs type stubs (a sibling .pyi if _writes.pyi or _statements.pyi would pass the size cap), capability-manifest entries (pgorm-python/src/statements/capabilities.rs), parity cases in checks/direct_builders.py, and live tests; the identifier oracle already covers the names, since Python reaches them only through the Rust APIs. python.statements gains MERGE and the versions in the same change."
                    }

