            {
                id python-campaign-entity-key-attributes
                title "Python's registered entities and the generative campaign reach an entity's temporal key and a relation's NOT ENFORCED, deferrability and PERIOD"
                kind @feat
                deps (entity-relation-not-enforced entity-temporal-keys)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-08T22:29:00.804794Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by entity-relation-not-enforced (cc6db4be) and entity-temporal-keys (832ed987), which extended the Rust entity layer and left Python's registered entities and the generative campaign's emitters as they were (both compile unchanged). What Rust now holds: RelationDef carries enforcement (Option<Enforcement>) and deferrability (Option<Deferrability>), set through RelationBuilder and DeriveRelation's enforcement = \"NotEnforced\" / deferrability = \"DeferrableInitiallyDeferred\", and schema generation writes them on the foreign key; a NOT ENFORCED relation's readers take an orphan as having no related row (load_one None, graph optional slot None, required slot drops the row). An entity's primary key may end WITHOUT OVERLAPS (#[pgorm(primary_key, without_overlaps)] on its last key field, PrimaryKeyTrait::without_overlaps()), looked up, updated and deleted by equality with the period part included; a relation may match on a PERIOD pair (ColumnPairs' period slot, RelationBuilder::period, DeriveRelation from_period / to_period), joining by overlap (&&), so a row reaches every version its period overlaps and load_one errors across two. Hypotheses to check, not facts: (1) a Python registered entity whose key has a Range part may not convert a Python range into the key's ValueType for find_by_id / delete_by_id (pgorm-python/src/entities); (2) describe() and the graph slots (pgorm-python/src/graphs/slots.rs) may not report a relation's enforcement, deferrability or period, and a PERIOD relation's graph slot would join by overlap with no Python-visible note; (3) the campaign's Rust emitters (security/generative) generate no relation with these attributes and no temporal key, so the generated corpus never exercises them. Acceptance: Python reaches a temporal-keyed entity by key and a PERIOD relation through a graph, with stubs and the wheel suites against 18.6; the campaign's emitters can declare each attribute (a btree_gist-requiring temporal key needs the extension in the fixture schema) with replay cases. Spec homes: python.entities, entity.relation.def, entity.traits.primary-key, generative.compile-suite."
                    }

