    {
        id created-range-types
        title "A range type a schema creates is named end to end: values over any subtype, its DDL read by codegen, its columns typed"
        kind @feat
        log (
            {
                at 2026-10-07T10:38:09.942226Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Split from range-consumers-beyond-builtins (2026-10-07) because it needs its own design: how pgorm names a range type a schema created. Pieces: (a) Range<T> over a subtype none of the six built-ins range over (CREATE TYPE floatrange AS RANGE (SUBTYPE = float8) would be Range<f64>); (b) codegen for CREATE TYPE ... AS RANGE, which sql_schema refuses by name (statement_kind), and columns of a created range type, which it cannot type today (codegen.ddl.types deliberately produces no ColumnType::Named). What a value of a created range needs, measured against the code: binding and decoding need only the subtype, because tokio-postgres reports a created range as Kind::Range(subtype) (live: `slot` over int4 binds and decodes through Range<i32> today). Three things need the created type's name, and Value::Range's tag (RangeType, the closed six) cannot hold it. The inline literal is the range type's constructor call (`floatrange(1.5, 2.5, '[)')`, `'empty'::floatrange`). A value in a position that types nothing renders `$1::<range type>` (sql.render.sql-json, sql.render.cast-param-type). An entity column's ColumnType must render the created name in DDL, while codegen must know the subtype to write Range<T>. Options weighed: (1) a RangeType variant holding a &'static descriptor. RangeType grows from 1 byte to 16, so ArrayType grows and Value doubles from 16 to 32 bytes. That is still inside range.rs's 4-word size assertion but against Value's one-pointer-payload doc. (2) a subtype-only tag, a new enum beside RangeType in which the six built-ins name their range. Value stays small and binding and decoding work. But the inline literal of a nameless range needs a textual `'[1.5,2.5)'` renderer with range_in's own quoting, which the constructor-call rendering exists to avoid, and its placeholders go untyped, so to_string() and SQL/JSON positions fall back to context typing. (3) a user-declared Rust type naming the created range (an ActiveEnum-like derive over a newtype), which still needs (1) or (2) underneath. Codegen also needs either a ColumnType that carries name and subtype (a new variant, touching every exhaustive consumer including JsonValueType's mirror) or a side channel out of parse_schema. A limitation that exists today, checked live on 18.6: a created range over a built-in subtype is tagged as the built-in, so to_string() writes `int4range(..)` where the column is `slot`. The server refuses it: INSERT is 42804 (\"column span is of type slot but expression is of type int4range\") and `slot = int4range` is 42883, because there is no cast between range types. Only the bound path is right."
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-07T15:22:46.401323Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Operator 2026-10-07, choosing between (a) growing pgorm's Value from 16 to 32 bytes so a value can carry its created range type's name, and (b) keeping Value at 16 bytes and rendering a created-range value as a textual range literal cast to the named type: \"(b)\". So Value does not grow. A value of a range type a schema creates (e.g. CREATE TYPE floatrange AS RANGE (SUBTYPE = float8)) binds or inlines as its text form ('[1.5,2.5)') cast to the caller-named type: a quoted, possibly schema-qualified Name, registered in the identifier oracle. The literal comes from a renderer that escapes bounds through the value pipeline and is round-tripped live against the server's canonical form. The to_string() bug that range-consumers-beyond-builtins found, writing int4range(..) for a column whose type is a created range over int4, is fixed on the same path. Codegen for CREATE TYPE ... AS RANGE belongs to this node."
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-07T15:40:07.457987Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

