    {
        id postgres-19
        title "pgorm builds what PostgreSQL 19 adds to its typed surface"
        kind @feat
        children (
            {
                id pg-query-19
                title "pgorm parses with PostgreSQL 19's grammar once libpg_query releases one"
                kind @build
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T15:47:52.370747Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by pg19-test-server's preliminary run on 19beta4 (branch pg-19, 2026-10-07). libpg_query has no 19 grammar yet: its newest tag is 18.1.0 and its branches stop at 18-latest, and pg_query.rs has nothing past its pg-18 branch (both checked with git ls-remote on 2026-10-07). pgorm emits no 19-only syntax, so the 18 grammar covers everything it writes today, and the whole suite passes on 19beta4 with it. Checked with pg_query::parse at pgorm's pinned fork rev 09ebc3a4, the 18 grammar refuses: INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (c) DO SELECT [FOR UPDATE] [WHERE ..] RETURNING; lag(x) IGNORE NULLS / first_value(x) RESPECT NULLS OVER (..); REPACK; WAIT FOR LSN; CREATE PUBLICATION .. FOR ALL TABLES EXCEPT TABLE. It parses SELECT 1 ignore, which 19 refuses (42601): 19 adds five unreserved keywords (ignore, lsn, repack, respect, wait; pg_get_keywords diffed between 18.6 and 19beta4), and ignore and respect are not bare column labels. pgorm writes AS before every column label, so no render is affected, but until the oracle parses with 19's grammar it would pass such a render. The restricted set (COL_NAME, TYPE_FUNC_NAME, RESERVED) is unchanged in 19, so sql.value's quoting and its keyword pin hold. Until this lands, sql! and prql! refuse 19-only syntax at compile time, metric fingerprints and the pipeline's census cannot read it, and codegen's parse_schema cannot read DDL that uses it. Every node that renders 19-only syntax depends on this one, so the identifier oracle parses what it renders. Route as pg-query-18 took it: move the necessary-nu/pg_query.rs fork to libpg_query's 19 tag once one is released (or take upstream's own 19 release), pinned by exact rev; the operator chooses between the fork and waiting. Acceptance: every pg_query user above parses 19's grammar, a 19-only statement passes sql!, sql.render.oracle's rationale names 19, and the oracle and every pg_query-based test pass."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T18:33:28.974072Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "sql.target (2026-10-07) holds every parser on PostgreSQL 18's grammar under either target until this lands — the render oracle, sql! and prql!, codegen's DDL reader, the paginator, metric fingerprints — and leaves to this node how they follow the pg-19 feature, on one condition: an 18 build's sql! must not accept what 18 cannot parse. A feature cannot swap one pg_query for another: features are additive, so an 18 grammar behind default plus a 19 one behind pg-19 would link both libpg_query copies, whose C symbols collide. The workable shapes are one grammar (19's) for every build, with an 18 build's sql! and oracle refusing the 19-only parse nodes (ON CONFLICT DO SELECT's action, a function call's null treatment, the new statements) by walking the tree; or a 19 libpg_query built with prefixed symbols. Whichever is chosen, pgorm-sql-macro and pgorm-codegen gain pg-19 then (no surface of theirs differs yet), and tests/target_release_tests.rs's CARRIERS list grows to match."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-02T13:13:52.110819Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "Waiting on an external release, re-checked 2026-11-02 with git ls-remote: libpg_query's newest tag is still 18.1.0, and its branches stop at 18-latest-dev (plus release-18-1-0 and update-to-postgres-18-6); there is no 19 grammar to build against. Nothing in this repo can move it, so it is marked blocked rather than left at the head of the frontier, where nplan_next kept offering it as the next node. Unblock when libpg_query tags a 19 release and pg_query.rs (or the necessary-nu fork) carries it."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id pg19-on-conflict-do-select
                title "An insert returns the row a conflict found: PostgreSQL 19's ON CONFLICT DO SELECT"
                kind @feat
                deps (pg-query-19 pg19-test-server)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T15:47:52.436363Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by pg19-test-server's preliminary run on 19beta4 (2026-10-07); release notes E.1.3.2. Checked live on 19beta4: INSERT ... ON CONFLICT <arbiter> DO SELECT [FOR UPDATE | FOR NO KEY UPDATE | FOR SHARE | FOR KEY SHARE] [WHERE <cond>] RETURNING .. returns the existing row for each proposed row that conflicts, beside the rows it inserts (a two-row insert with one conflict returns both, INSERT 0 2). Refusals: no RETURNING is 42601 (\"ON CONFLICT DO SELECT requires a RETURNING clause\"); no arbiter is 42601 (\"requires inference specification or constraint name\"), so it is a third ConflictAction under OnConflict::Targeted and never under AnyDoNothing; NOWAIT or SKIP LOCKED after the lock strength is a syntax error. ON CONSTRAINT works as the arbiter. WHERE filters which conflicting rows come back (a false filter returns none) and may read the target and excluded; RETURNING may not read excluded (42P01), and its old and new both read the existing row. It works as a CTE body. Builder: a ConflictAction arm carrying an optional lock strength and filter (sql.ast.on-conflict, sql.render), with the insert's RETURNING required by the type when the action is DO SELECT, so the refused insert does not construct ([dec:pgorm:invalid-states-unrepresentable]). ORM: a get-or-insert terminal on Insert (one model per input, inserted or found) is the natural consumer; for one row it replaces the ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING plus SELECT round trip. Python: python.statements' conflict handling gains the action, with capability-manifest entries, stubs and parity cases in checks/direct_builders.py. Depends on pg-query-19 (the oracle must parse what is rendered) and pg19-test-server (the live tests need a 19 server)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T18:33:28.67657Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Home under the pg-19 feature (sql.target, 2026-10-07). DO SELECT is syntax only 19 parses, so it is built behind #[cfg(feature = \"pg-19\")] in pgorm-query, marked doc(cfg(feature = \"pg-19\")), and pgorm-python's conflict handling gains it behind pgorm-python's own pg-19, its capability entry carrying \"features\": [\"pg-19\"] and absent without it. It cannot be a plain ConflictAction::DoSelect variant under cfg: ConflictAction is an exhaustive public enum, and a variant that appears whenever any crate in the graph turns pg-19 on breaks every exhaustive match a dependent wrote, which is the additivity sql.target forbids. Two homes stay additive: a typed path of its own reached from the targeted arbiter's typestate (OnConflict::Targeted), which is also where the type can require the insert's RETURNING; or ConflictAction made #[non_exhaustive] first, in the default build, as a separate breaking change. The first fits the RETURNING requirement. Its live tests are #[cfg(feature = \"pg-19\")] and run with cargo nextest run --workspace --features pg-19 against pgorm-test-19 (port 54319); the rust-pg-19 check platform keeps them compiling. Still behind pg-query-19: the oracle parses with 18's grammar and refuses the render."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id pg19-window-null-treatment
                title "A window function skips nulls: PostgreSQL 19's IGNORE NULLS and RESPECT NULLS"
                kind @feat
                deps (pg-query-19 pg19-test-server)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T15:47:52.501906Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Filed by pg19-test-server's preliminary run on 19beta4 (2026-10-07); release notes E.1.3.2. Checked live on 19beta4: lag, lead, first_value, last_value and nth_value take IGNORE NULLS or RESPECT NULLS between the call and OVER (lag(x) IGNORE NULLS OVER (ORDER BY ..)); RESPECT NULLS is the default. An aggregate refuses the clause (42809, \"aggregate functions do not accept RESPECT/IGNORE NULLS\"), as does any other window function (0A000, \"function row_number does not allow RESPECT/IGNORE NULLS\"), and the five still need OVER (42809). pgorm reaches these functions only through Func::named and expr_window (sql.ast.window-statement), and FunctionCall has no place for the clause. Weigh a typed home: constructors for the five functions that carry a null treatment, rather than a modifier on every FunctionCall that the server refuses for almost all of them. Python's pipeline and expression surfaces would follow the same shape. Depends on pg-query-19 (the oracle must parse what is rendered) and pg19-test-server (the live tests need a 19 server)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-07T18:33:28.843989Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Home under the pg-19 feature (sql.target, 2026-10-07). The typed home this note weighs — constructors for lag, lead, first_value, last_value and nth_value that carry a null treatment — is new items behind #[cfg(feature = \"pg-19\")], marked doc(cfg(feature = \"pg-19\")), and so additive by construction. The alternative is ruled out by more than the server's refusals now: a null-treatment field or variant added under cfg to FunctionCall or WindowFunction appears whenever any crate in the graph turns pg-19 on, and breaks a dependent's exhaustive match or struct literal. Python's expression and pipeline surfaces follow behind pgorm-python's own pg-19, their capability entries carrying \"features\": [\"pg-19\"]. Live tests are #[cfg(feature = \"pg-19\")] against pgorm-test-19; the rust-pg-19 check platform keeps them compiling. Still behind pg-query-19."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id python-acceptance-emcc-probe
        title "The acceptance runner's no-Python build probe passes on a host whose emcc is a Python program"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-10-08T15:47:43.547951Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found while running python-typing-check-symlinked-tmp's acceptance (2026-10-08). pgorm-python/checks/acceptance.py rust_without_python() builds the default workspace with python, python3, python3.14, pip, pip3 and maturin replaced by stubs that record their invocation, and fails if any ran. On a fresh target directory it fails on a macOS host with Homebrew's emscripten installed, for a reason that has nothing to do with pgorm: libc 0.2.189's build script runs `emcc -dumpversion` on every host (build.rs emcc_version_code(), not gated on the emscripten target), and /opt/homebrew/bin/emcc is a shell wrapper that runs `python3 -E .../emcc.py`, which hits the python3 stub. Reproduced with the stubs logging their parent: the invoker is target/debug/build/libc-*/build-script-build, cwd libc-0.2.189. The probe's intent is that pgorm's own build needs no Python tooling; a third-party build script probing for an unrelated compiler is outside it. Options: shadow emcc in the probe directory with a stub that exits non-zero without recording (libc then takes its non-emscripten default, as on a host without emscripten), or record the invoking process and ignore invocations whose parent is emcc. A warm target directory hides the failure because the build script does not rerun, so the probe should be tested against a fresh target. The 2026-10-08 acceptance record ran with an emcc that exits 1 first on PATH."
            }

