            {
                id napi-addon-size
                title "pgorm-napi's release addon ships stripped and as small as a release build makes it, down from about 23 MB"
                kind @build
                deps (napi-packaging)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-10-18T14:41:16.446465Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Measured on macOS arm64 (rustc 1.99.0), release addon as built / stripped (strip -x): release 23.2/18.4 MB; +lto=fat 17.8/14.6; +codegen-units=1 alone 14.9/12.1; fat LTO + one codegen unit 14.1/11.8; that + opt-level \"s\" 11.5/9.1; + \"z\" 10.4/6.8. Release builds already carry no DWARF (Cargo strips debuginfo when debug is off); stripping removes ~55,000 local symbols (4.8 MB). Speed, by instructions retired and cycles under /usr/bin/time -l (wall time was too noisy at load 40-110): decoding 500k rows, release 10.8-11.0G cycles, fat+cgu1 10.3-10.5G, \"s\" 10.8-10.9G, \"z\" 14.9G; compiling the PRQL parity pipelines x40, release 4.39G, fat+cgu1 4.11G, \"s\" 4.14G, \"z\" 7.87G. Decision: the `addon` profile is release + lto=\"fat\" + codegen-units=1 + strip=\"symbols\" with opt-level 3 kept, 11.8 MB and slightly faster than release; \"s\" would save another 2.6 MB for about 4% more cycles decoding, left to the operator; \"z\" is too slow. panic stays unwind. Packaged darwin-arm64 tarball 8.3 MB -> 5.3 MB. A linux-arm64 glibc container build of the profile is 12.3 MB, has no .symtab, keeps napi_register_module_v1 in .dynsym and loads under Node 26; linux-x64-gnu (28.7 MB unstripped, 21.2 MB stripped in CI's last run) is exercised only by CI."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-18T14:41:20.074691Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-10-18T15:47:46.057673Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator 2026-10-18, offered opt-level \"s\" for a further 2.6 MB (9.1 MB) at about 4% more cycles when decoding rows: \"speed\". The addon profile keeps opt-level 3."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id napi-listen-abort-race
                title "Aborting a JavaScript listener's in-flight listen always rejects it and closes the listener"
                kind @fix
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-11-03T18:09:52.13362Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Failed once in CI on PR #25 (linux-x86_64 / Node.js 26, run 41687791212), on code no lane touched; passed on the same code locally and in PR #24. pgorm-napi/tests/listen.test.ts \"an aborted pull leaves the listener open; an aborted listen closes it\": `const listening = listener.listen(\"napi_uncertain\", { signal: cut.signal }); cut.abort(new Error(\"cut off\")); await assert.rejects(listening, /cut off/)` got \"Missing expected rejection\", so the listen resolved. The abort fires synchronously, before any native completion can be delivered to JavaScript, so if the binding registered its abort handler before returning, the abort should always win. Look for the binding checking a native in-flight flag that the worker thread clears on completion, or registering the handler after an await, and make an abort that fires before the promise settles always reject it and close the listener (the LISTEN's effect is uncertain). Then repeat the test (e.g. 1000 iterations) to show the race is gone."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-05T00:52:38.750696Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-05T01:02:52.49869Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-10-09T10:47:56.15092Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Operator 2026-10-09: \"I want a `neon` crate so I can use this (just like the Python one) from Deno (yes, Deno supports the N-API)\", then, while the plan was being filed: \"it should be called pgorm-napi\". Decisions, the last two asked and answered:\n- The crate is pgorm-napi, its own cargo workspace and a cdylib, as pgorm-python is.\n- It is built with the Neon framework (neon-bindings, latest stable 1.x, napi-6 or lower) targeting Node-API; \"napi\" names the interface, Neon is the framework.\n- TypeScript declarations (.d.ts) are the .pyi stubs' counterpart.\n- The API mirrors pgorm-python's structure in JavaScript conventions: camelCase, Promises for anything async, typed errors.\n- Live tests run under BOTH runtimes, Node's test runner and `deno test` with the permissions a native addon needs (--allow-ffi, plus net/env/read as required). That is the operator's explicit exception to their global rule that only `deno check` is run in Deno; `deno check` remains the type check.\n- Local build first: build the .node and load it from a path. npm packaging with per-platform prebuilt binaries, importable from Deno as npm:, is napi-packaging, last.\n\nThe risk to retire first: Neon's async bridge (Channel, promises) and its drop_queue cleanup are built on napi_threadsafe_function. Deno reports Node-API version 10 and exports those functions, but others still treat their behaviour outside Node as unverified. napi-proof gates everything else on a live proof in both runtimes, including a clean process exit. Machine facts at filing: deno 2.9.5, node 22.15.0."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id supply-chain-detached-workspaces
        title "The supply-chain check audits the detached pgorm-python and pgorm-napi workspaces, and pgorm-python's TLS stack passes it"
        kind @fix
        log (
            {
                at 2026-10-09T13:05:04.850184Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Found while adding TLS to pgorm-napi (napi-connections, 2026-10-09). The supply-chain workflow runs `cargo deny check` over the root workspace only, after `cargo generate-lockfile` there; pgorm-python and pgorm-napi are their own Cargo workspaces with committed lockfiles, and nothing audits them. Run by hand with the root policy — `cargo deny --manifest-path pgorm-python/Cargo.toml check --config deny.toml` (cargo-deny 0.19.9 does not find the root deny.toml from a detached workspace by itself, so `--config` is needed) — pgorm-python fails three ways: (1) advisory RUSTSEC-2026-0285, rustls 0.23.44 accepts TLS 1.3 handshake messages across encryption-level boundaries, fixed in 0.23.45; (2) RUSTSEC-2025-0134, rustls-pemfile 2.2.0 is unmaintained (rustls-pki-types' PemObject replaces it); (3) license: webpki-roots 1.0.9 is CDLA-Permissive-2.0, which deny.toml does not allow. pgorm-napi passes at 0f95d938: it pins rustls 0.23.45, reads PEM through rustls-pki-types and takes roots from the platform through rustls-native-certs rather than webpki-roots. Acceptance: the workflow audits all three workspaces, each against its committed lockfile; pgorm-python passes (rustls bumped, rustls-pemfile replaced, and the webpki-roots license either allowed in deny.toml with a written reason or the bundled roots replaced — an operator decision, since it changes where pgorm-python's default trust comes from)."
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-09T13:20:37.732423Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Operator 2026-10-09, asked whether pgorm-python should keep its bundled webpki-roots, with CDLA-Permissive-2.0 allowed in deny.toml, or take its trust roots from the platform as pgorm-napi does: \"b\". pgorm-python moves to the platform's roots through rustls-native-certs, as pgorm-napi does at 0f95d938, and webpki-roots leaves its dependency tree; deny.toml's licence list is unchanged. That changes pgorm-python's default trust: a wheel trusts what the host's certificate store trusts, not a list bundled at build time. Say so in pgorm-python's TLS docs and DISTRIBUTION.md, including what a host with no usable certificate store gets. A caller-supplied CA keeps working as it does today. The rest of the node stands: rustls to >= 0.23.45 (RUSTSEC-2026-0285); rustls-pemfile replaced by rustls-pki-types' PemObject (RUSTSEC-2025-0134); and the supply-chain workflow audits the root, pgorm-python and pgorm-napi workspaces, each against its committed lockfile."
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-09T13:22:22.007203Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-09T13:39:40.322092Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-09T13:39:50.048551Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Done 2026-10-09. pgorm-python: rustls 0.23.45, PEM through rustls::pki_types::PemObject, roots from rustls-native-certs 0.8.4 (the versions pgorm-napi resolves); rustls-pemfile and webpki-roots are out of its graph and of the application-binding and generative bridge lockfiles, which depend on it by path. A store yielding no usable root (empty or missing SSL_CERT_FILE, empty SSL_CERT_DIR, a host with no CA bundle) fails Pool construction with ConstructionError naming what the loader reported; it never builds a pool that trusts nothing. New installed tests: the run's own CA is refused under the platform store, accepted through cafile and through SSL_CERT_FILE, and an empty/missing store fails construction while cafile still connects. Documented in the README's Connections section and DISTRIBUTION.md's new \"TLS trust\" section. The audit was widened to every committed lockfile (13 detached + the generated root). Beyond the three pgorm-python failures it found one more class: every unpublished fixture crate (python test fixtures, generative bridge/compile/replay, the five findings reproducers, the sqlmap adapter) failed `licenses` as unlicensed because it declared no licence. Fixed mechanically by declaring the repository's `MIT OR Apache-2.0`, as pgorm-napi (publish = false) and the codegen scaffold already do; replay.py's reproducer template now declares it too. deny.toml is unchanged. The workflow discovers lockfiles with `git ls-files`, audits each with `--locked` against deny.toml from one advisory-db fetch, reports every failing workspace before failing, and now also runs on pushes to main that touch a manifest, lockfile or the policy. Sensitivity: pinning rustls 0.23.44 back into pgorm-napi failed the audit with RUSTSEC-2026-0285 and the loop named only pgorm-napi; restored byte-for-byte. No nplan check platform for it: a commit gate failing on an advisory published against an unrelated dependency would block every manifest commit with no bypass, so auditing stays in CI (weekly, PRs, pushes to main)."
                after_done true
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id deps-refresh-2026-10
        title "Every workspace's dependencies move to their latest releases, majors included, and the minimum Rust version rises to what they need"
        kind @build
        deps (supply-chain-detached-workspaces)
        log (
            {
                at 2026-10-09T13:24:49.134666Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Operator 2026-10-09, after running `cargo update` on the root workspace: \"run cargo outdated for major versions as well, and update everything\". The lead read \"everything\" as including the minimum Rust version rise that uuid 1.27 needs (rust-version 1.88 → 1.89); flag it if anything else needs more.\n\n`cargo outdated --root-deps-only` survey, the same day:\n- Root workspace: uuid 1.26.1 → 1.27.0 only; everything else is current within MSRV 1.88 after the operator's update.\n- pgorm-napi:\n  - fallible-iterator 0.2.0 → 0.3.0 (breaking)\n  - tokio-postgres-rustls 0.13.0 → 0.14.0 (breaking)\n  - uuid 1.27.0\n  - jiff 0.2.38, tokio 1.53.2, tokio-util 0.7.20\n- security/generative/replay: fallible-iterator 0.3.0, jiff 0.2.38, tokio 1.53.2, uuid 1.27.0.\n- pgorm-python/tests/application-binding: pyo3 0.29.3, tokio 1.53.2.\n- pgorm-python/tests/codegen-entities: tokio 1.53.2.\n- security/generative/bridge: pyo3 0.29.3.\n- security/sqlmap/adapter: libc 0.2.190, tokio 1.53.2.\n- security/generative/compile and every findings reproducer: up to date.\n- pgorm-python: cargo-outdated 0.19 cannot read its manifest, failing on `pg-19 = [\"pgorm/pg-19\"]` through the path dependency, so survey it by hand (cargo tree, `cargo update --dry-run --verbose`, crates.io).\n\nCaution: fallible-iterator is part of postgres-protocol's and tokio-postgres's public API (row and array iteration). If the version they depend on is still 0.2, moving a crate's own dependency to 0.3 splits the trait in two. Check, and if 0.2 must stay, record why rather than forcing it. The same goes for tokio-postgres-rustls 0.14 against the rustls and tokio-postgres versions it requires.\n\nAcceptance: every workspace with a committed lockfile, plus the root manifests, at its latest compatible release, with majors taken where the dependency graph allows and each one that cannot be taken recorded with its reason. rust-version, the CI toolchain pins and the docs move together. The full suites pass: root nextest on 18.6 and with pg-19 on 19, pgorm-python's suite and acceptance, pgorm-napi in both runtimes, the generative unit tests, the replay and compile crates, and the sqlmap adapter's tests. The supply-chain audit of every workspace passes, along with the fork pin tests.\n\nRuns after supply-chain-detached-workspaces (rustls and rustls-pemfile in pgorm-python are that node's), and after any napi node in flight has committed, since both touch the same lockfiles."
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-09T13:49:15.069497Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-09T14:33:12.005171Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Done 2026-10-09 on branch deps-refresh. rust-version 1.88 -> 1.89 in all 13 manifests that carry one (uuid 1.27 needs 1.89; with CARGO_RESOLVER_INCOMPATIBLE_RUST_VERSIONS=allow nothing else in any 1.89-bound workspace moves, and `cargo +1.89 check --all-targets` passes for the root workspace, pgorm-napi, pgorm-python, replay and compile). Root floors: jiff 0.2.38, uuid 1.27, mac_address 1.2.0, unicode-ident 1.0.26, trybuild 1.0.122, pgvector ~0.4.2 -> 0.4.2. Operator follow-up (relayed): every exact pin removed. All `=` requirements in the seven detached workspaces became carets at the latest release (lockfiles hold the exact versions); git forks stay pinned by rev. Majors: tokio-postgres-rustls 0.13 -> 0.14 (napi, python), toml 0.9 -> 1.1 (pgorm-python build-dependency). Held: fallible-iterator 0.2 (postgres-protocol 0.6.12, the latest, exposes 0.2's FallibleIterator; reason commented in the three manifests). Behaviour change: 0.13 parsed the server certificate as a TBSCertificate, so channel binding was always none; 0.14 computes tls-server-end-point, so TLS+SCRAM now uses SCRAM-SHA-256-PLUS. New tests in pgorm-python (test_verified_tls_binds_scram_to_the_certificate) and pgorm-napi (skips without a password and CA, so CI's trust-auth cluster skips it) connect with channel_binding=require; against a 0.13 build the napi test fails with an authentication error. pgorm-python's capability binding.version comes from its lockfile via build.rs; check_artifacts compares the sdist lock's pyo3 with the checkout's; the scaffold writes carets with the installed pyo3. Tool pins: CI on stable Rust, Python 3.14, Node 26, Deno v2.x, uv and Maturin >=1.15,<2 unpinned, latest cargo-deny (0.20 CLI: root --config, cargo fetch + --offline replaces --disable-fetch), actions bumped to latest releases still pinned by SHA; nplan checks run ruff@latest (0.16.10) and mypy@latest (2.4.0); ruff 0.16's findings fixed in security/generative (BLE001 off for the package with reason); generative unit suite on python3.14 (PEP 758 syntax). hatchling >=1.27, psycopg[binary] >=3.3,<4 with installed versions recorded; the campaign build records Maturin's release. Left pinned deliberately: sqlmap pins.json (digest-verified archive, image digest, exact Python the harness compares). Acceptance: root nextest 1821/1821 on 18.6, 1822/1822 with pg-19 on 19beta4; doctests pgorm-query --all-features and pgorm; pgorm-python suite 178 OK and full acceptance passed; napi node 98 pass + 1 skip (no pgvector), deno 99 passed; sqlmap adapter 30 tests; fork pin tests 6/6; supply-chain audit 14/14 under cargo-deny 0.19.9 and 0.20.2."
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-09T14:33:12.059842Z
