    {
        id tx-closure-only
        title "Transactions are opened only through a closure: begin() is gone from the public API, savepoints and the metrics wrappers take the closure form, and the bindings run their transactions inside one"
        kind @feat
        deps (tx-closure-send)
        log (
            {
                at 2026-10-24T15:10:40.210184Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Operator 2026-10-24: once the closure forms run in spawned tasks, remove begin() (\"rewrite the bindings\", choosing deletion over deprecation, per the standing rule that a replaced API is deleted, not deprecated). Design, probed on Rust 1.99: TransactionTrait carries transaction<T, E, F>(&mut self, f: F) -> impl Future<Output = Result<T, E>> with F: AsyncFnOnce(&mut DatabaseTransaction<'_>) -> Result<T, E>, one default body, and no + Send on the returned future: a trait method promising + Send cannot prove the closure's CallOnceFuture is Send on stable (bounding it needs the unstable async_fn_traits; a helper trait with a Send associated future is not general enough for async closures), but without the promise the future of a call on a concrete type is Send, so a connection transaction with a savepoint transaction nested in it ran in tokio::spawn and committed. The cost: code generic over C: TransactionTrait gets a future not known to be Send. Scope: transaction on the trait for DatabaseConnection, DatabaseTransaction (savepoint) and InstrumentedConnection/InstrumentedTransaction (recording the begin/commit/rollback metrics the guard did); transaction_with and transaction_with_retry stay inherent on DatabaseConnection (a savepoint takes no mode); delete begin, begin_with, begin_instrumented and the public commit/rollback (the closure decides: Ok commits, Err rolls back, a dropped future rolls back); pgorm-migration's exec_with_connection moves to the closure; pgorm-python (transactions/native.rs, scope.rs) and pgorm-napi (connect/transaction.rs) run each transaction's command loop inside transaction(async |tx| ..), returning Ok on the commit command and Err on rollback, a nested Begin recursing through tx.transaction, with their Python and JavaScript APIs unchanged; the 62 begin() calls in tests move to closures, and the guard-drop tests become tests that a failed or dropped closure rolls back. Update conn.tx, conn.tx.guard, conn.tx.closure, metric.layer.tx, the bindings' transaction rules, CLAUDE.md's Scoped transactions bullet and the Coming from SeaORM guide. Delete origin/feature/transaction-blocks only if the operator says so."
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-24T15:48:03.232328Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-25T20:15:32.414478Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "As built (Rust 1.99 probes): the closure's handle is `&mut Self::Transaction<'_>`, the type declared on a crate-private `sealed::Handle` supertrait — DatabaseTransaction on a connection or transaction (the decided signature exactly), InstrumentedTransaction on the metrics wrappers, so statements and savepoints inside an instrumented transaction stay instrumented (a fixed DatabaseTransaction handle lost that and left InstrumentedTransaction unconstructible). Opening is `sealed::Open: Handle` on the parent, ending `sealed::Close` on the handle, every method taking a Token only pgorm constructs; no public hook. Rejected by probe: `Open<'t, P>` implemented on the handle (an impl relating handle and borrow lifetimes fails a spawned Send check, which erases them: 'not general enough'), and the type on the opening trait itself (forces `where Self: 't`, which a savepoint closure's higher-ranked bound cannot meet). Per the lead, a failed ROLLBACK is no longer only logged: TransactionError::RollbackFailed { cause, rollback } carries it, retry stops on it, migration discards its connection, and both bindings map it to an error reply plus discard, their behaviour before the change."
            }
        )
    }

