            }

            {
                at 2026-10-30T14:24:19.463958Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Reads done after binding-coverage-per-impl (2026-10-30): bindings.quantifiers still states today's behaviour, which both bindings cover. The work starts spec-first: bump bindings.quantifiers to the operators PostgreSQL reads a quantifier after (established against the server and pgorm-query's renderer), which leaves both bindings' annotations stale and the node open until both land."
                after_done true
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-31T23:54:15.23845Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Done 2026-11-01 (46c24ea8 spec, a05fddac feat). Established on PostgreSQL 18 (pgorm-test): ANY/ALL follow = <> < <= > >=, LIKE / NOT LIKE / ILIKE / NOT ILIKE (no ESCAPE after the quantifier: 42601), ~ !~ ~* !~*, @> <@ &&, << >> &< &> -|-, @@ and @? (whose array the server types jsonpath[], so a bound text array is a ConstructionError as it runs and one cast to jsonpath[] is sent); SIMILAR TO, IS [NOT] DISTINCT FROM, BETWEEN and IN are 42601; + - * / % and || are 42809. pgorm-query renders binary(op, Func::any/Expr::any) bare for each, so it needed no change. bindings.argument-errors' example \"any(..) and all(..) outside a comparison\" now reads \"where no operator takes one (bindings.quantifiers)\" without a bump: no argument-errors site changes behaviour, and a bump would re-pin about 28 sites the sibling branches also touch. Left for a follow-up: pgorm-query's Func::any / some / all docs and sql-ast.md's eq-any paragraph still say a quantifier renders only after a comparison."
                after_done true
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id binding-variant-coverage
        title "Each binding's tests prove it handles every variant of the open enums it translates"
        kind @test
        deps (public-enums-non-exhaustive)
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-10-26T18:06:31.299236Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found by public-enums-non-exhaustive (81eeeac8). Marking pgorm-query's open enums #[non_exhaustive] means a variant added later no longer breaks the bindings' builds: their wildcard arms compile, and the missing spelling shows only at run time as an unsupported error. non_exhaustive_omitted_patterns, the lint that would catch it, is still unstable on Rust 1.99. Give each binding (and the generative replay harness) a test that walks every variant of each open enum it translates (BinOper, Value kinds, ColumnType, the JSON behaviours and so on: whichever each binding matches over) and fails if one reaches the wildcard arm. pgorm-query needs a way to enumerate the variants (an ALL constant, or a test-only iterator); choose the one that does not make the enums' variant lists a second public API to keep in sync, and say why."
            }

            {
                at 2026-10-31T22:54:45.333805Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-01T01:22:41.069947Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "The enumeration is pgorm-query's tests_cfg::Variants, behind the hidden tests-cfg feature that sql.surface already keeps out of the surface: variants() gives one value of each variant (payloads a binding translates, never SQL NULL), variant_name() names it, and missing_from(&[Self]) names the variants a binding's spellings leave out. Each impl is generated by one macro from one list, and variant_name's match over the list is exhaustive inside pgorm-query, so a list missing a variant does not build and one naming a variant twice trips unreachable_patterns. A public ALL constant was rejected: it would be a second spelling of each open enum that callers could depend on, maintained by hand and growing as a semver surface with every variant; a derive was rejected because a generic payload (an empty name, a ColumnType subtype of Char) would not reach the arm a binding translates a real one through. The bindings and the replay harness take pgorm-query as a dev-dependency with tests-cfg. Covered: Python and JavaScript walk ArrayType (every kind), Value, ColumnType (the set a registered column has no kind for is pinned to Bit, VarBit, Money, Named, LTree), the four SQL/JSON behaviours and BinOper (spelled, or named as left out); JavaScript also round-trips every kind through Value in its live suite via a native valueKinds export; the replay harness encodes every Value. Not walked: wildcards that select one shape (SimpleExpr::FunctionCall in FROM, SqlJson aggregates before OVER, FromItem::Table in a relation's describe, ColumnSpec refusals in ALTER), pgorm::Error's classification (its default class is the rule), and the enums the bindings only construct from spellings other than BinOper and the JSON behaviours."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-01T03:02:50.884953Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

