    {
        id python-conflict-merge-shapes
        title "Python names conflict targets with Conflict.on and MERGE actions with MergeAction, as JavaScript does"
        kind @refactor
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-11-01T02:30:55.365373Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Decisions (D) and (E) of the operator's 2026-11-01 answer (binding-spec-consolidation). (D) In pgorm-python: Conflict.on(*targets) names an inference target of columns and index expressions, as pgorm-napi's Conflict.on does; ConflictTarget stays exported for annotations but is not constructible; ConflictUpdate.update(*columns) takes several, as JavaScript's update(...columns). (E) MergeAction.update(column, value), .insert(column, value), .delete(), .do_nothing() and .insert_defaults() as pgorm-napi's MergeAction factories; MatchedAction and NotMatchedAction deleted; MergeUpdate and MergeInsert not constructible; an action given to the wrong kind of arm is a ConstructionError, as bindings.merge requires (Python raises TypeError today, breaking it); the stubs keep the matched and not-matched arms' types apart so mypy still refuses a wrong pairing. Update python.naming (it still says ConflictTarget(columns)), the shared rules, bindings-surface.json's shapes entries, docs, typing cases and the generative campaign."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-01T11:20:16.633915Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Delivered (D): Conflict.on(*targets) makes an inference target of columns and index expressions; ConflictTarget has no constructor (stub __init__ takes Never); ConflictUpdate.update(*columns) takes one or more, none a ConstructionError as on a ConflictTarget. (E): MergeAction.update/insert/delete/do_nothing/insert_defaults make every MERGE action; MatchedAction and NotMatchedAction are deleted; MergeUpdate and MergeInsert have no constructor; an action on the wrong kind of arm is a ConstructionError, a non-action still a TypeError. Choices: MergeUpdate and MergeInsert are not runtime subclasses of MergeAction (JavaScript's are), since a subclass would show the five factories as their members to the surface test; the stubs make MergeAction generic in a contravariant phantom row type, delete() a MergeAction[Literal[\"target\"]], insert_defaults() a MergeAction[Literal[\"source\"]] and do_nothing() a MergeAction[Literal[\"target\", \"source\"]], as CopyFrom's format is, so mypy refuses every wrong pairing (rejection cases added). Rules bumped: bindings.writes+2 (Conflict's factories, the three classes not callable, columns named once, an unrunnable write refused as a WITH clause reads it), bindings.merge+2 (MergeAction's factories, the three classes not callable), python.naming+3 (the exceptions gone; a class a shared rule makes by a factory alone is not called), python.typing+2 (the row type parameter), napi.naming+4 (the exceptions gone); every annotation in both bindings re-pinned. Capability keys conflict.target became conflict.on and merge.update/merge.insert became merge_action.update/insert/delete/do_nothing/insert_defaults. bindings-surface.json shares Conflict.on and MergeAction and the shapes question no longer lists conflict arbiters or MERGE actions. JavaScript gained tests that its conflict and MERGE action classes cannot be called. The campaign's emitters and dispatchers write p.Conflict.on(..)."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-01T12:27:44.044976Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id binding-statement-defects
        title "A JavaScript write read into a WITH clause is checked as it would be run, pgorm-query writes AS MATERIALIZED with one space, and Python's Insert.columns refuses a second call as JavaScript's does"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-11-01T02:30:55.565366Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found by python-select-gaps / python-statement-gaps (merged 4896da7e). (1) pgorm-napi's src/statements/with.rs takes an INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE body without that write's built() checks, so a write that could not run (no rows, nothing set, no where / all_rows) sits in a CTE and fails only at the server, against bindings.statements; Python refuses it as the clause reads it. (2) pgorm-query renders a CTE's materialization as `AS  MATERIALIZED` with two spaces; fix the renderer and its goldens. (3) pgorm-python's Insert.columns called twice appends to the Rust column list, where pgorm-napi refuses a second call; refuse it in Python as a ConstructionError, as JavaScript does, with a test in each."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-01T03:07:24.696175Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-01T11:14:14.05758Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Fixed all three. (1) pgorm-napi's write.rs gives Insert, Update and Delete each a check() that built() and with.rs's expression() both read, so a WITH clause refuses a write that could not run with the ConstructionError inspecting it gives (test \"a write that could not run is refused as a WITH clause reads it\" in tests/writes.test.ts). (2) pgorm-query writes `MATERIALIZED ` / `NOT MATERIALIZED ` after `AS `; sql.render.cte moved to +5, whose text spelled the leading space, with a golden in tests/postgres/query.rs and the two-space strings in pgorm-napi/tests/parity/select.json and pgorm-python/tests/test_cte_options.py fixed. (3) Python's Insert.columns refuses a second call as JavaScript's does, tested in both. No shared rule's text changed for (1) or (3): bindings.statements already requires a method to refuse an argument it cannot use."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-01T12:27:44.145896Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id binding-graph-describe-shapes
        title "A registered graph's and a source tuple's describe() report the same keys in both bindings, held by the surface test"
        kind @refactor
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-11-01T02:30:55.824297Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found by binding-describe-shapes (merged 61f70c15), which settled a model's and an entity's describe() keys: a registered graph's describe() and a source tuple's still differ between the bindings, Python's carrying keys JavaScript's lack (terminals, aliases, cursor, result). Settle one key set each under decision (6), change the side that differs, state them in bindings.entity-graphs and bindings.pipeline-sources, and declare them under bindings-surface.json's descriptions so each surface test holds them, run against the application modules as the entity descriptions are."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-01T10:48:43.544049Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Settled under decision (6), JavaScript's sets, spelled by each binding's naming rule. Registered graph: {name, rust_shape, sources: [{index, entity, slot}]}, slot root, Req or Opt. Source tuple: {name, rust_shape, entities}. Python dropped its constants: a graph's terminals [\"all\", \"optional\", \"cursor.all\"], aliases (a fixed sentence) and cursor (a fixed object), and a source tuple's terminals [\"all\", \"one\", \"optional\"] and result (a fixed sentence); JavaScript unchanged. Neither the generative campaign nor Python's codegen read those keys (codegen embeds the described registrations in _COMPATIBILITY and compares them at load, so a module generated before this change refuses to load until regenerated). bindings.entity-graphs+3 and bindings.pipeline-sources+3 state the shapes; bindings.surface+2 extends descriptions to both; bindings-surface.json declares pgorm.EntityGraph and pipeline.SourceSelection; each surface test compares every registered graph's and source tuple's describe() with them, skipped in the standalone modules and run against the application modules by checks/entities.py and checks/entities.js. Bite proof: renaming EntityGraph's rust_shape to rust_type made Python's test fail ('rust_shape: undeclared', 'rust_type: missing') and JavaScript's fail in Node and Deno ('rustShape: undeclared', 'rust_type: missing'); restored, both pass."
