            {
                id disjoint.evolve
                title "A kind can be added to or removed from an existing disjoint entity"
                implements {
                    main ([spec:pgorm:sem:disjoint.evolve] [spec:pgorm:req:migration.commit-after])
                }
                deps (disjoint.schema)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-11-02T12:32:26.486249Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Scope: no rule yet; write it when this node starts and put it to the operator before code. What it has to settle: adding a kind needs two transactions, because PostgreSQL cannot use a value ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE added inside the transaction that added it (first the value, then the kind table, the base's generated key columns and the foreign keys); removing a kind has no ALTER TYPE ... DROP VALUE to use, so the kind type is recreated and the kind column retyped, refused while any base row still has that kind; a unit kind gaining a table, or a payload kind losing one, is the same column-and-constraint change on an existing value. Every name involved is deterministic per disjoint.schema, so the statements can name what they alter. Decide whether this is Schema statements a migration runs, a pgorm-migration helper, or both, and how up() splits the two transactions."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-03T10:23:48.026317Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator, 2026-11-03 (\"yes to both\"), on the lead's proposal. Adding a kind has two routes: by default ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE, then a commit, then the new kind's table, generated key columns and foreign keys — which needs a pgorm-migration opt-in for a migration that commits before the next runs, since up() runs a whole batch in one transaction and PostgreSQL cannot use an added enum value before its transaction commits; and, for a single atomic migration, recreating the kind type in one transaction (a new enum with every value, both kind columns retyped, the disjoint constraints and generated columns dropped and recreated, the old type dropped and the new one renamed), at the cost of rewriting the base and every kind table under an exclusive lock. Removing a kind always takes the recreate route (no ALTER TYPE ... DROP VALUE) and is refused while any base row has that kind. A unit kind gaining a table adds its foreign key NOT VALID, leaves the backfill to the migration, then VALIDATEs. A kind losing its table drops data, so only when asked explicitly. The rule goes to the operator before code."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-03T14:28:27.666874Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Probed on PostgreSQL 18.6 before code. Confirmed as the rule says: ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE ... AFTER/BEFORE, then using the value in the same transaction is 55P04 (\"unsafe use of new value\"), in the next one fine; the recreate sequence works end to end (temporary enum, drops, retype USING kind::text::new, DROP TYPE, RENAME, rewrite); a CHECK (kind <> 'v') refuses while rows hold the value (23514); a NOT VALID foreign key holds new rows at once and VALIDATE refuses while a row is missing; a schema-qualified quoted type (\"Book Shop\".\"Shelf Kind\") takes every statement. Two facts the code follows inside the rule's own words: (1) a kind table's kind column is a generated column, and PostgreSQL refuses USING on one (\"cannot specify USING when altering type of generated column\") while no cast joins two enum types (\"cannot be cast automatically\"), so the recreate retypes it by dropping it with its foreign key and writing it again of the new type — it is among \"the generated columns dropped first and written again after\"; (2) a default on the base's kind column blocks the retype (\"default ... cannot be cast automatically\"), so the recreate drops it before and sets the declaration's again after the rename. pgorm-query has no ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE ... USING and no DROP DEFAULT; both are added to sql.ddl.alter-table. remove_disjoint_kind_table also takes the table it drops, which the declaration as it will be no longer names; the spec text says so."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id disjoint.one-of
                title "A reference can point at exactly one of several entities"
                implements {
                    main ([spec:pgorm:def:disjoint.one-of])
                }
                deps (disjoint.write)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-11-02T12:32:26.575268Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Scope: no rule yet; write it when this node starts and put it to the operator before code. Sketch from the 2026-11-02 discussion: a reference whose type is an enum, Commentable::Post(post_id) / Commentable::Photo(photo_id), stored as one nullable foreign key column per variant plus a CHECK that exactly one is set (num_nonnulls(...) = 1); \"attached to nothing\" comes from the enum, as a unit variant or an Option around it, and relaxes the CHECK to at most one. Open: whether the model carries the raw columns with the enum as a typed accessor or one field spanning several columns (pgorm maps a field to one column today), how load_one / the graph resolve it to the target's model, and what codegen recognises. A target may itself be a disjoint base."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-03T10:23:50.700413Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Operator, 2026-11-03 (\"yes to both\"), on the lead's proposal: the referencing model keeps one plain nullable foreign-key column per target, declared as ordinary belongs_to relations (pgorm maps a field to one column, and every existing path keeps working); #[derive(OneOf)] on an enum types the choice over those columns (#[pgorm(entity = comment::Entity)], each variant #[pgorm(column = Column::PostId)] naming its column and its target entity); reading gives the chosen target's key, setting it on the active model sets that column and clears the others, and loading gives the target's model, one query per target table as load_one does; \"attached to nothing\" comes from the enum, a unit variant relaxing the schema's CHECK (num_nonnulls(...) = 1) to <= 1; codegen recognises nullable foreign-key columns covered by exactly such a CHECK."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-03T16:39:25.935207Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Rule revised to +1 where the code forced it or the rule left it open. (1) load_one gives None for a key whose target row is absent, so the rule's \"the error load_one gives an unmatched key\" described nothing; OneOf::load now raises Error::Query itself, naming the table, the row's key, the target's key and the column. (2) The CHECK is named ck-{table}-{enum}, with the enum's name in snake_case and bounded through derived_name. It is named for the enum, not the columns, so codegen, which names the enum for the constraint, gets back the enum the schema was written from. (3) The column's type is checked at compile time through a new ColumnField<const COLUMN: usize> trait. The Model derive implements it for each field, keyed by `Column::X as usize` (macros.derive.model +6). (4) Loading reads each target through the entity's Related<T>, so a target with no Related relation does not compile, and neither do two variants naming one entity (E0119, one OneOfTarget impl per target). load refuses a Related relation that joins a column other than the variant's, as Error::Type before anything is sent. (5) key_attrs(..)/model_attrs(..), as kind_attrs; <Enum>Key derives Clone, Debug, PartialEq and <Enum>Model derives Clone, Debug. (6) Codegen: the DDL bridge now reads table-level CHECKs (within the DEFAULT expression subset) onto the statement (codegen.ddl.tables +12, codegen.ddl.unsupported +20), which reverses the earlier \"CHECK stays refused\" note. This is needed so that near misses (>= 1, an extra conjunct) generate plain fields as the rule says, rather than failing. An unnamed CHECK is named as PostgreSQL names it. The unit variant is None. Name clashes are refused."
                    }
                )
            }

