                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The enum's name survives in the constraint's name, not in a comment and not by renaming from the targets. A CHECK's name is scoped to its table (pg_constraint keys it by relation), so the table the constraint is on already says what `ck-{table}-` repeats. Schema::create_one_of names the CHECK `ck-{table}-{enum}` when that is under 63 bytes, as now, and otherwise `ck-{enum}`; codegen reads a name under 63 bytes on table T as enum `rest` when it is `ck-T-{rest}`, or when it is `ck-{rest}` and `ck-T-{rest}` would not have been under 63 bytes (so create_one_of would have written the short form), and the whole name otherwise. Both readings are exact for every name create_one_of writes, need no hash to verify, and leave every name it writes today unchanged. The one case `ck-{enum}` cannot hold, an enum whose snake_case name passes 59 bytes, the OneOf derive refuses at compile time, as the unique(name) and enum-label limits are refused; a hand-written OneOf with such a name gets the bounded `ck-{enum}` and codegen names it for the whole name. Rejected: naming from the targets when a 63-byte name is seen (the enum's name is lost, and create_one_of on the generated enum writes a different constraint, so the schema no longer round-trips); COMMENT ON CONSTRAINT (a second statement from create_one_of, a comment surface the bridge and TableCreateStatement do not have, and a user-owned comment repurposed)."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-05T00:16:45.052411Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id codegen-check-not-dropped
                title "Codegen never generates an entity that silently drops a table CHECK it read"
                kind @fix
                deps (disjoint.one-of)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-11-03T17:47:46.640992Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "From disjoint.one-of (e21257e2), raised with the operator for a decision: to make the rule's near misses generate as plain fields, codegen.ddl.tables+12 / codegen.ddl.unsupported+20 now read any table-level CHECK within the DEFAULT expression subset and carry it on the statement, but the entity can't hold it, so create_table_from_entity on the generated entity writes the table without it. This reverses the 2026-10-08 decision \"CHECK stays refused (the entity holds none)\". Two ways out: (a) refuse every CHECK that is not exactly the one-of shape, as before, and make the near-miss tests expect the refusal, the rule's \"ordinary nullable fields\" then meaning columns no CHECK covers; or (b) let an entity declare CHECKs (a check(..) container attribute written by create_table_from_entity), so codegen keeps every CHECK it reads. Recommended: (a) now, (b) as its own feature if wanted. Waiting on the operator's choice."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-03T22:13:57.193916Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Lead, 2026-11-03, pending operator override: take (a). Codegen refuses every table CHECK that is not exactly the one-of shape, restoring the 2026-10-08 decision \"CHECK stays refused (the entity holds none)\" that disjoint.one-of reversed without approval. The near-miss tests expect the refusal, naming the constraint; the rule's \"ordinary nullable fields\" means columns no CHECK covers. Revise codegen.ddl.tables / codegen.ddl.unsupported and disjoint.one-of to say so. Option (b), entities declaring CHECKs, is not this node."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-04T23:38:20.782437Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-11-04T23:48:45.903544Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-11-02T12:32:26.300001Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Operator, 2026-11-02, over two discussions. The enum describes the rule: a payload variant is a kind whose row must exist, a unit variant is a kind with no table, and a base row with no kind row is legitimate exactly for the unit kinds (the operator corrected the lead's first assumption that it is always a mistake). No wide or single-table mapping, ever (\"we're not doing wide tables\"). \"Belongs to one of\" references are in scope, built after subtypes (disjoint.one-of). A variant whose row may be missing (Car(Option<Details>)) is out: a unit kind that becomes a payload kind once its row exists covers it. Codegen first recognises the shape pgorm writes (disjoint.codegen); kind-less existing schemas come later and read-only (disjoint.codegen-unkinded). The new derive is born under the derives rework's conventions, hence disjoint.declare's dep on derives.boilerplate."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-02T12:32:26.397462Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Constraint design probed on PostgreSQL 18 (pgorm-test, scratch databases, dropped), 2026-11-02. Shape: base UNIQUE (id, kind); each kind table a STORED generated constant kind column and an FK (id, kind) to the base, ON DELETE CASCADE; on the base one STORED generated column per payload kind (CASE WHEN kind = 'car' THEN id END) with an FK to the kind table. All 11 cases held under both INITIALLY DEFERRED and DEFERRABLE INITIALLY IMMEDIATE: a car with its row in one WITH statement ok; a car with no row, a truck row for a car, a vehicle with car and truck rows, truck->bike leaving the truck row, and deleting a car's row alone each refused; bike as a base row alone, car->truck / truck->bike / bike->car each in one statement, and deleting a vehicle (cascading its row) ok. Under INITIALLY IMMEDIATE a violation inside a transaction is reported at the offending statement, not at COMMIT, and SET CONSTRAINTS ALL DEFERRED still lets hand-written multi-statement changes through, so disjoint.schema requires IMMEDIATE. STORED must be explicit: 18 defaults generated columns to VIRTUAL, and a virtual column refuses the enum (\"generation expression uses user-defined type\"). Triggers stay out (sql.scope)."
            }
        )
    }

