            }

            {
                at 2026-11-02T21:25:35.861185Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-02T21:33:42.598018Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Kept Decimal and made it work, with no new Value variant: ColumnTrait's default casts read a money column (an array of it, a domain over either) as numeric and write a value cast to money (to the domain by name; money[]/domain[] for arrays and = ANY operands). The cast pins the placeholder to the value's own type (sql.render.cast-param-type), so a Decimal binds as numeric and PostgreSQL converts at lc_monetary's scale both ways; a value left numeric would assign but not compare (42883 money = numeric). Rejected the i64-minor-units newtype: binding it needs its own Value variant (a BigInt accepted at a money placeholder would mean minor units where PostgreSQL's int8->money cast means whole units), threaded through pgorm-query's Value (Display, identity/hash, kind, ArrayType tag, JSON), pgorm's bind/decode/COPY paths and both bindings' value tags, and its inline literal cannot be rendered at all without the server's frac_digits. Not done here: the bindings' entity kinds still report money as Explicit (a tagged decimal Value works); a pipeline ExprOps comparison on a money column binds bare, as it does for every save_as column."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-02T21:34:31.695407Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id created-range-subtype-typmod
        title "A created range's subtype carries no type modifier PostgreSQL ignores"
        kind @fix
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-11-02T14:25:05.129542Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Found by codegen-compact-string-length (fdd065f7), confirmed live: CREATE TYPE r AS RANGE (SUBTYPE = numeric(10,2)) is accepted, but PostgreSQL drops the type modifier, so the range's subtype is plain numeric. Codegen's DDL bridge keeps the typmod in the CreatedRange subtype, so the generated entity claims a precision the database does not hold. Read the subtype without its typmod (or refuse it, saying PostgreSQL ignores it); check pgorm-query's CREATE TYPE ... AS RANGE builder for the same claim."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-02T20:58:39.220045Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-02T21:07:37.586029Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Refuse, not read without. The bridge's rule is that the file is never quietly reinterpreted, and it already names the other modifiers the server drops or reduces (a sized array bound, an interval precision past 6); reading numeric(10,2) as numeric would be that reinterpretation. `char`/`character` without a length reach the bridge as bpchar(1) (the grammar's implicit modifier) and are refused too; `bpchar` is the unbounded subtype they range over. The described-schema gate (validate_col_type) also refuses a CreatedRange whose subtype carries a modifier (Decimal(Some), Char(Some), String(N)). pgorm-query's RangeDefinition renders the subtype as given; sql.ddl.type-range already states PostgreSQL discards the modifier, and a new live test (tests/created_range_tests.rs) builds one over numeric(10, 2) and witnesses pg_range's subtype as numeric with bounds of any scale, so the builder makes no claim beyond its rendered text and was left unchanged."
            }

            {
                at 2026-11-02T21:09:53.793138Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }
        )
    }

